#!/usr/bin/env bash
# gh-shim/gh — the argv-level admin-merge guard (#984).
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. The review-enforcer answers *"will this command text make gh
# perform an admin merge?"* from STRING rules, and seven adversarial rounds each
# closed one splice and found the next (`--ad""min`, `--ad\min`, `$'--admin'`,
# `$'merge'`, `xargs {}`, `-${V:--}admin=true`, `$V-admin=true`). That class is not
# closable in a scanner, and the reason is a proof rather than an impression:
# resolving `$VAR`/`$(…)` requires EVALUATING a shell, which a gate cannot do
# without running the very command it exists to refuse.
#
# This shim answers the same question from its OWN argv — a layer where the shell
# has already finished. `-${V:--}admin=true` is the literal string `--admin` by the
# time this process starts, so there is no splice left to hide behind and no string
# rewriting to anticipate. The scanner stays as the fast first layer (it produces
# better messages and costs nothing); THIS is the layer that does not share its
# limit.
#
# Installed ahead of the real gh on PATH by the review-enforcer's `tool_call` hook,
# so enforcement is not something a caller can forget.
#
# RULE: an admin merge is allowed only with a head-bound certifying evidence comment
# for the CURRENT head. Everything else — every ordinary gh command — is passed
# through untouched.
#
# THE FLEET COURTESY GATE (#1910). Every call — including the admin-merge path — also
# passes a per-machine pacing gate before it runs. WHY: the account's *secondary*
# (concurrency) limit refuses calls while the primary counter still reads healthy, so
# `rate_limit` cannot see the thing that is biting (see STATED LIMITS and the gate
# block below). Measured 2026-10-17: five boxes' controllers plus hand-dispatched
# workers, each issuing `gh` calls with nothing coordinating them, and the account
# refused every GraphQL call while `used: 541` of 5000.
#
# THE PR POSITIONAL (#1007). gh takes a NUMBER, a URL, or a BRANCH in this slot. This
# layer resolves a bare number and exactly ONE URL spelling —
# `https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>/pull/<digits>` — and refuses every other value,
# including a branch. That URL shape is safe to accept because the number is IN the
# token: no second party is asked who the PR is. A branch selector is
# refused DELIBERATELY, not for want of effort: resolving a branch to a number means
# calling `gh`, i.e. a gate asking the thing it is gating for the identity it is about
# to check — at which point the gate no longer has an independent answer. That refusal
# is an over-block, and an over-block is a real defect (it is what pushes a caller at
# AGENT_ADMIN_MERGE_OVERRIDE), so it is stated here and pinned by a test rather than
# left as a surprise. An accepted URL is not waved through: it is resolved to a number
# and routed through the FULL gate — head pinning, evidence, zero residual — exactly
# as a bare number is.
#
# PARITY. The scanner (`extensions/review-enforcer/index.ts`, `extractMergeSelector` —
# which `extractMergePrNumber` now delegates to) runs FIRST and accepts the SAME one URL
# shape, so a caller sees one answer, not two. The case tables that pin this are
# `tests/gh-shim/run.sh` §11 and `index.test.ts` ("extractMergeSelector: …"); §11(g) is
# a tripwire that fails if the TS shape moves without this file being revisited.
#
# STATED LIMITS — WHAT THIS LAYER CANNOT SEE (the counterpart to the scanner's own
# STATED LIMITS, which this layer backs rather than replaces):
#   - `gh alias`. The argv here is `gh <alias> …`; gh expands the alias AFTER we
#     have run, so an alias defined as `pr merge --admin` is invisible to BOTH
#     layers. Named, not silently trusted.
#   - a command that discards this PATH entry — `env -i … gh …`, or an explicit
#     `PATH=` reset — and a caller that invokes the real gh by ABSOLUTE path.
#     Both are deliberate acts to remove the guard rather than subtle ones to hide
#     a token, which is the improvement this layer buys.
#   - argv already expanded in a PARENT process the harness never sees.
#   - it is only active where it is wired: the review-enforcer puts it on PATH for
#     bash tool calls, so a merge issued from outside the harness is not covered.
#   - the CERTIFICATE IS NOT AUTHENTICATED. It is checked for shape, bound to the
#     head, and required to be non-vacuous — but it is comment TEXT, and the same
#     token that would merge can also write the comment. An agent that types the
#     certifying lines by hand satisfies this gate. That is why the post-merge
#     detector, not this gate, is the authority of record; this gate raises the
#     cost of an unevidenced merge, it does not prove one was earned.
#   - `gh api graphql -f query=@file` / `--input <file>` / `-F query=@-`. The mutation
#     text is read from a FILE or STDIN, so no argv token carries `mergePullRequest`.
#     A file- or stdin-supplied query is therefore refused outright whenever the call is
#     `gh api graphql`.
#   - AGENT_GH_REAL and AGENT_ADMIN_MERGE_OVERRIDE ARE CALLER-CONTROLLED. Both are the
#     "caller deliberately removes the guard" class — the same class as `env -i`, a
#     `PATH=` reset, or invoking the real gh by absolute path. They exist because a
#     nested call (the verifier shelling out to gh) must not re-enter this shim. What
#     this layer defends against is a caller removing the guard WITHOUT MEANING TO — by
#     splicing a token, or without knowing the guard is there. It is not, and cannot be,
#     a defence against the caller deciding to remove it; the post-merge detector is what
#     makes THAT visible after the fact, and AGENT_ADMIN_MERGE_OVERRIDE announces itself.
#   - a BRANCH selector in the PR position (`gh pr merge my-branch --admin`) is refused
#     rather than resolved (#1007) — see THE PR POSITIONAL above. This is an over-block
#     and it is deliberate.
#   - a non-`github.com` PR URL (GitHub Enterprise, `www.github.com`, an explicit port,
#     a case-variant host) is refused rather than resolved (#1007). The host cannot be
#     forwarded to the evidence lookup, so accepting it would read the evidence from a
#     DIFFERENT host's repo — the `--repo` wrong-repo class in a new spelling.
#
# STATED LIMITS OF THE COURTESY GATE (#1910) — that layer's own, not the argv layer's:
#   - it paces the callers that go THROUGH this shim. A caller that removes the PATH
#     entry (the class above), or that resolves the real binary by ABSOLUTE PATH, is not
#     paced. `scripts/queue-dispatch.py` did exactly that, deliberately and read-only; it is retired
#     (agent-infra#1874) and lives at `scripts/orchestrator-harness/retired/queue-dispatch.py`, so no
#     live caller does it today (`docs/ops/gh-rate-limits.md` states the same).
#   - it is per MACHINE and per USER (`~/.pi/agent/gh-gate`). The ceiling it protects is
#     shared across the whole ACCOUNT, including boxes it cannot see, so the cap here is
#     a courtesy between boxes, not a total: it can only be well under the real ceiling.
#   - a shim killed with SIGKILL while its `gh` child is still running releases its slot
#     by PID reaping (the child is not counted). Concurrency can therefore exceed the cap
#     by at most the number of children orphaned that way, until they exit.
#   - a mutex `mkdir` is a best-effort lock: a process killed between `mkdir` and writing
#     its pid is stolen after the stale window below, and a steal can race a re-acquire.
#     The failure mode is a momentary over/under-count, never a permanent deadlock.
#     A mutex whose pid file cannot be WRITTEN is released and reported unavailable
#     rather than entered unowned.
#   - ⛔ NOTHING HERE MAY TOUCH A SUCCESSFUL CALL'S STDERR. The harness layer ABOVE this one
#     (`gh-rest/gh`) captures `2>&1` and PARSES the merged bytes as JSON; a pacing banner on a
#     good answer turns `pr view --json` into a JSONDecodeError, and on the `pr merge` path it
#     reports FAILURE for a merge GitHub already performed. So pacing notes are appended to
#     `$GATE_DIR/events.log` ALWAYS, and replayed to stderr only when this call finally FAILS
#     (where the caller is already printing stderr). The same rule is stated by gh-rest/gh and
#     gh-limit.py; it is restated here because this is the layer that violated it.
#   - a `retry-after` that `[ ]` cannot hold (more than 9 digits) is treated as ABSENT rather
#     than clamped: the comparison would error (an errored test reads FALSE), the `*1000` would
#     wrap, and the wrapped value would be written into the SHARED cooldown — parking every lane
#     on the box. `cooldown_remaining_ms` additionally drops any deadline further ahead than
#     `COOLDOWN_ABS_MAX_S` (**86400 s, a FIXED constant** — deliberately not a per-lane knob, so
#     one lane's `RETRY_MAX` cannot delete a window the whole box is waiting on), so a poisoned
#     file heals on the next read.
#   - ⛔ THE SHARED COOLDOWN IS RE-DERIVED BEFORE IT IS SLEPT ON (see `discharge_window`). A window
#     is EVIDENCE WITH A WINDOW, not a state: it was written by one refusal on one endpoint, and
#     nothing used to check it again. MEASURED 2026-11-01: three `gh run view` refusals wrote a
#     box-wide 60 s then 120 s window while the exempt `/rate_limit` read showed core 4,684/5,000 —
#     so every lane's every read queued behind a window no bucket justified (`gh api rate_limit`
#     timed out at 90 s; the same read direct took 0.432 s). A SECONDARY/abuse window is never
#     discharged this way (it is invisible to `/rate_limit`), and a revalidation that cannot
#     complete leaves the window intact — absence of evidence is never a discharge.
#   - ⛔ AND IT IS BOUNDED BY ONE BUDGET (`AGENT_GH_GATE_WAIT_BUDGET`, default 120 s), because an
#     unbounded wait here sits under a retry arm that sleeps 60 s + 120 s of its own and under
#     `gh-limit.py`'s 4 attempts: a ceiling that outlives its derivation. Past the budget the call
#     returns rc=75 with `gh-contended` on stderr — a distinct verdict, so "I could not ask" never
#     collapses into "the answer is empty".
#   - the retry is per WHOLE COMMAND. Below `gh-limit.py`'s own 4 attempts this MULTIPLIES: a
#     call can execute up to 3 (this gate) x 4 (that layer) times, including non-idempotent
#     creates and comments — the transport arm below does NOT add to a WRITE (it is retried zero times
#     on a transport failure), so a write's bound stays 3 x 4 = 12. For a READ the arms are additive:
#     see the transport bullet (5 x 4 = 20). "The tighter layer wins" is true for concurrency and for
#     a single wait, and FALSE for attempts. A caller that declared its own bound suppresses the
#     RATE-LIMIT wait entirely (`AGENT_GH_GATE_NO_WAIT=1`, or gh-limit's `GH_LIMIT_NO_WAIT=1`); it
#     does NOT suppress the transport retry's 1 s + 3 s, which is two orders below those budgets and
#     is the whole fix for a bounded caller that would otherwise lose the call to a dropped socket
#     (see the transport bullet).
#   - `AGENT_GH_GATE_SLOT` is FORGEABLE: any caller that can set its environment may point it at
#     an existing directory and take no slot at all. It is an INTERNAL inheritance seam between
#     this shim and the child it spawns, not a security boundary — the same class as
#     `AGENT_GH_GATE_MAX_INFLIGHT`, which a caller can also set.
#   - a slot whose recorded PID was later REUSED by an unrelated long-lived process is not
#     reaped by the liveness probe, so admission waits out `AGENT_GH_GATE_ACQUIRE_TIMEOUT` and
#     then runs the call ungated. That is a bounded per-call penalty, not a wedge — the fail-open
#     arm is what keeps it from becoming one.
#   - the retry is decided from the TEXT of stderr, because that is all `gh` leaves. It
#     fires only on the rate-limit signatures below and never on a bare 403, so a
#     permissions error is not retried (a mutating command must not run twice). A
#     genuinely rate-limit-shaped text that is NOT one — impossible to distinguish in
#     principle from the real thing — can cost one extra attempt.
#   - ⛔ THE TRANSPORT RETRY (#2148) IS A *SECOND* RETRY ARM, AND THE THIRD ATTEMPT MULTIPLIER.
#     It exists for the failure measured on 2026-10-24: two lanes died to ONE network blip
#     (`Connection error.` -> rc=1 -> lane EXIT), and three identical `gh api rate_limit` calls
#     two seconds apart on cell-1 returned ok / ok / `error connecting to api.github.com` with
#     2431 of 5000 requests still in hand — a per-CALL, intermittent transport fault, which is
#     the shape a single-shot call cannot survive and a retry can. It is bounded at 3 attempts
#     with a 1 s then 3 s backoff (<= 4 s added latency). ⛔ THE ARMS ARE ADDITIVE, NOT
#     MULTIPLICATIVE: they share ONE loop, so a single shim invocation issues at most
#     1 + (MAX_ATTEMPTS-1) + (TRANSPORT_ATTEMPTS-1) = 5 executions — and `gh-limit.py` above this
#     layer may make 4 of those invocations, so the real worst case for the SAME READ is 20.
#     STATED, not silent, and READS only (next bullet).
#   - ⛔ A MUTATION IS RETRIED ZERO TIMES ON A TRANSPORT FAILURE. A dropped connection may mean the
#     write SUCCEEDED (GitHub applied it, then the socket died), and a retry would then perform it
#     twice — two comments, two merges, two releases. The classification is argv-level (this
#     layer's whole advantage: `-X POST` is ONE token by now) and it is a WHITELIST: `gh api` is
#     retried only when it is provably a GET (no `-X`, no `-f/-F/--field/--raw-field/--input`), and
#     any `graphql` spelling is refused — `graphql`, `/graphql`, or a full
#     `https://api.github.com/graphql?query=…` URL. A query can BE the `mergePullRequest` mutation
#     and a file/stdin body cannot be classified at all (#984), so the match is on the WHOLE
#     positional, not a prefix. Other commands are retried only when the verb is one of gh's read
#     verbs; an UNRECOGNISED shape is NOT retried — its failure mode is one flaky-call failure,
#     exactly the pre-change behaviour, never a write run twice.
#   - ⛔ AND ITS RETRY LINE NEVER TOUCHES A SUCCESSFUL CALL'S STDERR, for the reason the pacing
#     notes do not (see the ⛔ above `gate_note`). The line is written to `events.log` on EVERY
#     retry (greppable: `gh-shim: transport retry 1/3`) and replayed to stderr only when the call
#     finally FAILS — which is exactly the case that kills a lane, so the lane log still shows it;
#     a call that RECOVERS leaves stderr byte-clean, which is what keeps every `--json` read above
#     this layer parseable (`gh-rest/gh` does `2>&1 | jq`). This is a deliberate departure from a
#     reading of #2148 that asked for a stderr line per retry: that line would turn every recovered
#     call into a JSONDecodeError upstairs, i.e. it would deliver the retry and lose its benefit.
#     Pinned in both directions by `tests/gh-shim-retry/run.sh` §1/§2 and `tests/gh-shim/run.sh`
#     §14(a).
#   - ⛔ STDOUT IS CAPTURED PER ATTEMPT, AND ONLY THE ATTEMPT WHOSE RC IS REPORTED IS REPLAYED. That is
#     the other half of a correct retry, not a buffering preference: `connection reset` and
#     `unexpected eof` leave EARLIER BYTES on stdout, so a streamed retry of `gh api --paginate`
#     returns rc=0 with page 1 from the FAILED attempt in front of the whole successful answer —
#     duplicated/half-written JSON, a double-counted tree in `atomic-land.sh`, and a false failure in
#     any `--json` reader. Consequences to know, all stated rather than discovered: (a) the capture
#     is a FILE in the gate workdir for the life of the call, so a command that PRINTS A SECRET
#     (`gh auth token`) now touches disk where it used to be streamed — created `0600` inside the
#     `0700` gate dir and removed on every exit path this layer controls (the exit trap, TERM/INT/HUP),
#     and it survives a SIGKILL, exactly as the pre-existing STDERR capture already does; (b) a call
#     whose stdout is a TERMINAL is still captured (gh sees a file, so its own colour/pager
#     autodetection is off and output arrives at exit) — a terminal on STDIN is what selects the old
#     streaming, never-retried branch, unchanged by this layer; (c) an output larger than the disk is
#     now a real bound on the call.
#   - ⛔ `rc=1` WITH EMPTY STDERR IS *NOT* A TRANSPORT FAILURE, though #2148's brief listed it. gh
#     exits 1 silently ON PURPOSE (`cmdutil.SilentError`) exactly where the answer is a VERDICT:
#     `gh pr checks` when checks FAILED, `gh run view --exit-status` when the run failed. Both are
#     whitelisted reads and `pr checks` is one of the fleet's hottest, so retrying that shape would
#     re-run every red PR three times and write a false `transport retry` cause into events.log. The
#     failures actually MEASURED (2026-10-24) all print, so the text signature is the whole test.
#     `tests/gh-shim-retry/run.sh` §8 pins the absence.
#   - pacing adds latency by design: the default is at most 2 calls in flight and 250 ms
#     between starts, so ~50 sweep calls cost ~12 s instead of ~0 s. That trade is the
#     point of the gate; `AGENT_GH_GATE_INTERVAL_MS=0` widens the throughput at the cost
#     of the burst protection.
#   - a non-INTERACTIVE call's stderr is captured and replayed after it finishes (that is
#     the only way to read a `retry-after`), so stderr can be reordered relative to
#     stdout. A call with a terminal on both stdin and stderr (a human at `gh auth login`)
#     is passed through un-captured and un-retried instead — a captured prompt would look
#     like a hang.
#
# Env:
#   AGENT_ADMIN_MERGE_OVERRIDE=1   the existing audited hatch: allow, loudly
#   AGENT_GH_GATE_DIR              the per-machine gate state dir (default `~/.pi/agent/gh-gate`)
#   AGENT_GH_GATE_MAX_INFLIGHT     max concurrent `gh` processes per machine (default 2)
#   AGENT_GH_GATE_INTERVAL_MS      min ms between call STARTS (default 250; 0 = no spacing)
#   AGENT_GH_GATE_MAX_ATTEMPTS     total attempts incl. the first, on a rate-limit refusal (default 3)
#   AGENT_GH_GATE_RETRY_BASE       seconds to wait when stderr carries no `retry-after` (default 60, floor 1)
#   AGENT_GH_GATE_RETRY_MAX        ceiling on any single wait, in seconds (default 900)
#   AGENT_GH_GATE_ACQUIRE_TIMEOUT  seconds to wait for a free slot before running UNGATED (default 300)
#   AGENT_GH_GATE_NO_WAIT=1        never sleep in-shim: return a rate-limit refusal at once
#                                  (gh-limit's GH_LIMIT_NO_WAIT=1 is honoured the same way)
#   AGENT_GH_GATE_WAIT_BUDGET       seconds the shim may spend waiting out a SHARED rate-limit
#                                  window before returning `gh-contended` rc=75 (default 120)
#   AGENT_GH_GATE_REVALIDATE        1 = re-derive an open shared window with one exempt
#                                  `/rate_limit` read before sleeping on it (default 1; 0 = old
#                                  behaviour, sleep on the recorded deadline)
#   AGENT_GH_GATE_REVALIDATE_FLOOR  core `remaining` at or below which a window is CREDIBLE and is
#                                  honoured without discharge (default 100)
#   AGENT_GH_GATE_LOCK_TIMEOUT_MS  ms to wait for the gate's own mutex (default 2000)
#   AGENT_GH_GATE_SLOT             INTERNAL: the slot a nested call inherits (never counted twice)
set -uo pipefail

# Resolve THIS script's own directory through any symlinks. The harness injects a
# NEUTRAL symlink (`~/.pi/agent/shims/gh` -> here) so the path it puts on PATH carries
# no gate-relevant word; `dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}"` would then yield the shim LINK's
# directory, the verifier would resolve to a path that does not exist, and every admin
# merge would be refused. Fail-closed, but the layer would be dead in production.
self="${BASH_SOURCE[0]}"
while [ -L "$self" ]; do
  self_dir="$(cd "$(dirname "$self")" && pwd)"
  self="$(readlink "$self")"
  case "$self" in /*) ;; *) self="$self_dir/$self" ;; esac
done
SELF_DIR="$(cd "$(dirname "$self")" && pwd)"
# NO override: this path is derived from the script's own location, so a caller cannot
# redirect the check at a file of its choosing. An earlier `AGENT_GH_SHIM_VERIFY` env
# seam was reachable in PRODUCTION — `AGENT_GH_SHIM_VERIFY=/dev/null` made the check
# exit 0 and the admin merge proceeded unevidenced, with no announcement (VGATE).
VERIFY="$SELF_DIR/../verify-admin-merge-evidence.sh"

# The real gh. `AGENT_GH_REAL` is set by an OUTER invocation so that a nested call
# (the verifier shells out to gh itself) cannot recurse back into this shim.
#
# ⛔ THE INHERITED VALUE IS NOT TRUSTED ON ITS OWN (#2266). The seam's whole purpose is
# that a NESTED call does not recurse — but the value it carries is whatever an outer
# process exported, and `find_real_gh` used to accept it on `-x` alone. One launcher in
# the fleet exported `AGENT_GH_REAL` pointing AT THIS SCRIPT (it had resolved it the same
# way), every descendant inherited it, and each generation re-`exec`ed the shim: a new
# process every `AGENT_GH_GATE_ACQUIRE_TIMEOUT` (300 s) because each parent then waited for
# a gh slot its own child was holding. Measured 2026-10-21: 2,543 shim processes, 2,460 of
# them older than 20 minutes, load average 12.8, and EVERY gh call on the box deadlocked for
# ~10 hours — the `-ef` skip below could not save it, because the env short-circuited before
# the PATH walk that performs that check.
# So a value that IS this script is refused, exactly like the `-ef` skip refuses a PATH hit
# that is this script, and resolution falls through to the PATH walk. Fail-closed in the
# same direction the skip already is: it can only send the call to a real gh.
# ⛔ ONE IMPLEMENTATION, BOTH HALVES (#1988, review of #2295). The PATH walk and the env
# seam make the SAME decision — "is this file a harness gh layer?" — so they must not each
# carry their own copy of it: the `-f` half of this seam was fixed precisely because the two
# halves of one decision had drifted apart. No pipe: `head | grep -q` can report failure
# through SIGPIPE under `pipefail`, and a false negative here means recursion.
_is_harness_layer() {  # a harness `gh` layer (`gh-rest`/`gh-limit`): its own sentinel line
  # ⛔ NO COMMAND SUBSTITUTION OVER A BINARY (#2184/#2305, review of #2295). `$(head -c 8192 …)` on
  # a 42 MB ELF makes bash >=4.4 print `warning: command substitution: ignored null byte in
  # input` to THIS shim's stderr on a call that SUCCEEDS. Pre-fix the env seam read nothing, so
  # that warning is newly reachable on every delegated call (the inherited value IS the genuine
  # gh) — and #2305 is precisely a gate that grades stderr. `grep -a` reads a binary safely and
  # prints nothing.
  # ⛔ REDIRECTION, NOT AN OPERAND, because the path is caller-controlled. `-f`/`-x` accept a
  # cwd-relative name beginning with `-`, and an operand is passed to the reader: without `--` a
  # value of `-q` became a grep OPTION, and even WITH `--` the lone value `-` still means STDIN to
  # `head`/`grep`, so the shim STALLED on a held-open stdin (both measured rc=124 — the incident's
  # own class, reachable from the value this seam newly reads). A redirection operand is always a
  # PATH, so `-` and `-q` both name the file the operator meant.
  # ⛔ THE WINDOW IS 8 KiB, NOT THE WHOLE FILE (review of #2295). A whole-file scan widens the
  # marker window: this shim's own sentinel sits OUTSIDE the first 8 KiB (pinned by §15(d)), so it is
  # not a harness layer — which makes the `-ef` self-refusal redundant (so removing either half keeps
  # the suite green), and puts this shim at odds with gh-rest/gh-limit, which both test the first
  # 8 KiB. It also cost 0.17 s per call over the 42 MB ELF on the hot path. 8 KiB keeps all three
  # consumers agreeing, keeps `-ef` the clause that refuses self-recursion, and keeps this shim
  # out of its own marker window — which is what lets gh-rest still delegate mutations through us.
  # Process substitution, not a pipe: its status is not in `$?`, so no SIGPIPE-under-`pipefail`.
  # ⛔ AND THE REDIRECTION'S OWN FAILURE IS THE CALLER'S STDERR (#2305, review of #2295). A failed
  # `< "$1"` is reported by the SHELL, not by `head`, and redirections apply LEFT TO RIGHT — so
  # with `head -c 8192 < "$1" 2>/dev/null` an unreadable candidate (mode 111, an ACL/FUSE deny-read)
  # prints `shim: line N: <path>: Permission denied` onto a call that SUCCEEDS (measured 82 bytes at
  # rc=0), which is `gh-rest/gh`'s `2>&1 | jq` JSONDecodeError — the very class this seam was fixed
  # for. `2>/dev/null` FIRST makes fd 2 /dev/null before the shell attempts the open. §10(a5) pins it.
  LC_ALL=C grep -qa 'harness-gh-shim-marker' <(head -c 8192 2>/dev/null < "$1")
}
find_real_gh() {
  # ⛔ `-f` TOO. `-x` is true for a SEARCHABLE DIRECTORY, so a directory-valued
  # AGENT_GH_REAL was accepted here and `exec`'d as the real gh — rc 126 "Is a directory"
  # on EVERY call. The PATH walk below has always carried `-f`; the env seam did not, so
  # the two halves of one decision disagreed (review of #2295, sibling fix in
  # scripts/orchestrator-harness/gh-rest/gh).
  # ⛔ AND A HARNESS LAYER IS REFUSED HERE TOO (#1988, review of #2295). An inherited
  # AGENT_GH_REAL naming `$O/gh-rest/gh` or `$O/gh-limit/gh` was accepted as "the real gh", so
  # the call re-entered the shim chain and the harness's own depth guard ended it with rc 127
  # for EVERY gh call in the tree — the same total outage the walk's marker skip exists to
  # prevent, reached through the inherited value instead of PATH (measured by review of #2295).
  if [ -n "${AGENT_GH_REAL:-}" ] && [ -f "${AGENT_GH_REAL}" ] && [ -x "${AGENT_GH_REAL}" ] \
     && ! [ "${AGENT_GH_REAL}" -ef "$SELF_DIR/$(basename "$self")" ] \
     && ! _is_harness_layer "${AGENT_GH_REAL}"; then
    printf '%s' "$AGENT_GH_REAL"; return 0
  fi
  local d
  local old_ifs="$IFS"
  IFS=:
  for d in $PATH; do
    case "$d" in "$SELF_DIR"|"") continue ;; esac
    [ -x "$d/gh" ] || continue
    # SKIP ANY DIRECTORY WHOSE `gh` IS THIS SCRIPT. Skipping only $SELF_DIR is not
    # enough: the harness prepends a NEUTRAL directory holding a SYMLINK back to this
    # script, so `-x "$d/gh"` was true for the shim itself, `REAL` became the shim, and
    # `exec "$REAL"` re-entered forever — EVERY gh call hung (VGATE cycle 3). `-ef`
    # compares device+inode THROUGH symlinks, so it catches a link, a link chain and a
    # hardlink alike.
    [ "$d/gh" -ef "$SELF_DIR/$(basename "$self")" ] && continue
    # ⛔ AND SKIP THE HARNESS'S OWN `gh` LAYERS (#1988). `$O/gh-rest`/`$O/gh-limit` are interposers
    # that resolve the real gh themselves by walking PATH; resolving one of THEM as "the real gh"
    # re-enters the shim chain and the harness's depth guard ends it with rc 127 for EVERY gh call in
    # the process tree (measured: `gh pr view` → 127, so a reviewer could not post its evidence).
    # They identify themselves with one sentinel line; this shim deliberately does NOT carry it, so
    # the harness layers still delegate MUTATIONS through here and #984's argv-level admin-merge guard
    # stays in force. No pipe: `head | grep -q` can report failure through SIGPIPE under `pipefail`.
    [ -f "$d/gh" ] || continue
    _is_harness_layer "$d/gh" && continue
    printf '%s' "$d/gh"; IFS="$old_ifs"; return 0
  done
  IFS="$old_ifs"
  return 1
}

REAL="$(find_real_gh)" || {
  printf 'gh-shim: cannot find the real gh (searched PATH minus %s)\n' "$SELF_DIR" >&2
  printf 'gh-shim: refusing to run an unresolvable gh — pass AGENT_GH_REAL if it lives elsewhere\n' >&2
  exit 127
}
# ⛔ AND REFUSE AT THE EXEC BOUNDARY TOO (#2266). `find_real_gh` is not the only way REAL can
# end up being this script — a PATH entry appearing between the walk and the exec, or a future
# caller of this seam, would reach the same recursion. One check here makes the SAME-INODE class
# unreachable rather than merely unlikely, and it costs one `-ef`. NOTE, honestly: this clause is
# DELIBERATELY UNREACHABLE from the finder today (both halves test the same device+inode), so no
# deterministic case can pin it — it exists for a race between the walk and this exec, and the green
# suite is NOT evidence that it works (a `if false` mutant is bit-identical to it; review of #2295).
# It is defence in depth, kept cheap and loud on purpose. A refusal is loud: silently
# re-entering is the failure that took the box's gh out for ten hours without a single error.
# ⛔ SCOPE, stated honestly: `-ef` is device+inode, so a literal COPY of this script is a DIFFERENT
# file and is NOT refused here (nor by the walk's `-ef` skip). Copies are reachable in production —
# `extensions/review-enforcer` falls back to `fs.copyFileSync` when symlinking the neutral entry
# fails — so two copies on PATH can still ping-pong, one hop per gate slot. The marker scan does
# not rescue a copy either: the sentinel sits outside the first 8 KiB by design (so that this shim is
# never classified as a harness layer, which is what keeps gh-rest delegating mutations through us),
# so `-ef` cannot see a copy and the marker test will not either. That class
# needs a sentinel of its own inside that window plus a hop-depth backstop: the depth-bound half
# tracked in #2304, deliberately NOT duplicated here, so this change stays the env-seam fix it is.
# …and a value that is not a REGULAR FILE is refused here too, for the same reason: `-x`
# is true for a directory, and exec-ing one turns every gh call into rc 126. Cheap, loud, and
# in the same fail-closed direction as the self-recursion refusal below.
if ! [ -f "$REAL" ]; then
  printf 'gh-shim: REFUSING to exec %s — not a regular file (`-x` is true for a directory, and exec-ing one fails rc 126 on every call)\n' "$REAL" >&2
  printf 'gh-shim: unset AGENT_GH_REAL, or point it at the genuine gh binary (e.g. /usr/bin/gh)\n' >&2
  exit 127
fi
if [ "$REAL" -ef "$SELF_DIR/$(basename "$self")" ]; then
  printf 'gh-shim: REFUSING to exec the shim as the real gh (%s) — that is a self-recursion, not a real gh\n' "$REAL" >&2
  printf 'gh-shim: unset AGENT_GH_REAL, or point it at the genuine gh binary (e.g. /usr/bin/gh)\n' >&2
  exit 127
fi
export AGENT_GH_REAL="$REAL"

# Resolve a PR URL to `<owner> <repo> <number>`, or return non-zero when the token is
# not EXACTLY one accepted URL shape (#1007). Strictness is the point: `…/pull/123`
# is accepted because the number is in the token, and every near-miss is refused
# because a gate must not guess at a route-equivalent spelling.
#
# Refused, each for a stated reason:
#   - any host but `github.com` — the host cannot be carried into `--repo`, so the
#     evidence lookup would run against a different host (see STATED LIMITS).
#   - a query or fragment (`…/pull/123?s=1`, `…/pull/123#x`) — gh routes both to the
#     same PR, but "route-equivalent" is not "the same string".
#   - an extra path segment, incl. a trailing `/` (`…/pull/123/files`, `…/pull/123/`) —
#     same reason, and it is how a `/pull/1/../2`-style confusion would enter.
#   - a non-numeric number, an empty owner/repo, a bare `github.com/…` without a
#     scheme. `http://` is NOT upgraded to `https://` here: a gate normalising input
#     is a second parse that can disagree with gh's.
#   - an owner/repo containing anything outside `[A-Za-z0-9_.-]` — GitHub's own repo
#     alphabet, and the EXACT character class the scanner's `PR_URL_RE` uses. The two
#     layers must accept the same set, or one over-blocks what the other accepts.
#   - a BRANCH name. See THE PR POSITIONAL: resolving one needs a `gh` call.
parse_pr_url() {
  local u="$1" rest owner repo num
  case "$u" in
    *\?*|*\#*) return 1 ;;
    https://github.com/*) ;;
    *) return 1 ;;
  esac
  rest="${u#https://github.com/}"
  owner="${rest%%/*}"
  [ -n "$owner" ] || return 1
  [ "$owner" = "$rest" ] && return 1          # no `/` after the owner
  case "$owner" in *[!A-Za-z0-9_.-]*) return 1 ;; esac
  rest="${rest#*/}"
  repo="${rest%%/*}"
  [ -n "$repo" ] || return 1
  [ "$repo" = "$rest" ] && return 1           # no `/` after the repo
  case "$repo" in *[!A-Za-z0-9_.-]*) return 1 ;; esac
  rest="${rest#*/}"
  case "$rest" in
    pull/*) num="${rest#pull/}" ;;
    *) return 1 ;;
  esac
  case "$num" in
    ''|*[!0-9]*) return 1 ;;
    # No leading zero: GitHub never emits one, and `Number()` on the TS side would map
    # `01006` to `1006` while this side passed `01006` to the verifier — a divergence in
    # the very value both layers exist to agree on.
    0*) return 1 ;;
  esac
  # At most 15 digits. Every number this short survives a JS `Number` exactly, so the
  # scanner (`PR_URL_RE` + its safe-integer check) accepts the SAME set; a longer one
  # would be rounded there and read a DIFFERENT PR's evidence. No real repo is within
  # 14 orders of magnitude of this bound.
  [ "${#num}" -le 15 ] || return 1
  printf '%s %s %s' "$owner" "$repo" "$num"
}

# Flag values that belong to gh's GLOBAL flags, so the walker does not mistake a
# repo name for the command word. (`--repo` is also the flag the verifier needs.)
# Value-taking flags of `gh pr merge` plus gh's globals. A VALUE must never be
# mistaken for the PR positional: `gh pr merge --admin --body 123` would otherwise
# verify PR #123 while the real command merges the CURRENT BRANCH's PR (VGATE).
VALUE_FLAGS=' -R --repo --hostname -b --body -F --body-file -t --subject -A --author-email --match-head-commit '
# Flags that take NO value. Anything else is UNRECOGNIZED, which makes the parse
# untrustworthy: we cannot tell a future flag's value from the PR number.
BOOL_FLAGS=' --merge -m --squash -s --rebase -r --delete-branch -d --auto --disable-auto --admin --help -h '

CMD=""; SUB=""; PR_NUM=""; IS_ADMIN=0; REPO_FLAG=""; PR_URL_REPO=""
seen_cmd=0; expect_value=0; prev_flag=""; saw_unknown_flag=""
for tok in "$@"; do
  if [ "$expect_value" -eq 1 ]; then
    expect_value=0
    tok_value="${tok#=}"
    case "$prev_flag" in -R|--repo) REPO_FLAG="$tok_value" ;; esac
    continue
  fi
  # ATTACHED forms carry the value in the same token, so they must be matched
  # BEFORE the generic `-*` branch — `--repo=owner/other` and `-Rowner/other` are
  # both accepted by gh, and dropping the repo reads the evidence from the WRONG
  # repository (VGATE). `-R=owner/other` is also valid pflag.
  case "$tok" in
    --repo=*) REPO_FLAG="${tok#--repo=}"; continue ;;
    -R?*)     REPO_FLAG="${tok#-R}"; REPO_FLAG="${REPO_FLAG#=}"; continue ;;
    # Attached SHORT values (`-b123`, `-Ffile`) are the same as `-b 123`; they consume
    # nothing further, and must not later be read as the positional.
    -b?*|-F?*|-t?*|-A?*) continue ;;
    # A bare `--` is an argument separator, never the positional.
    --) continue ;;
    # An ATTACHED value of a global flag. `--hostname=github.com` was refused as an
    # unrecognized flag (over-block, VGATE cycle 3).
    --hostname=*) continue ;;
  esac
  case "$tok" in
    -R|--repo|--hostname) expect_value=1; prev_flag="$tok"; continue ;;
  esac
  case "$tok" in
    -*)
      # An admin flag is matched HERE, on resolved argv, where `--admin` is a
      # literal string — the same value the scanner had to guess about.
      case "$tok" in
        --admin) IS_ADMIN=1 ;;
        --admin=false|--admin=FALSE|--admin=False|--admin=0|--admin=f|--admin=F) ;;
        --admin=*) IS_ADMIN=1 ;;
      esac
      # An ATTACHED value (`--body=123`) consumes nothing further. `--hostname=` was
      # missing here, so a legitimate global flag was read as an UNKNOWN flag before the
      # positional and the whole command was refused (#1007 VGATE cycle 2 — an over-block,
      # exactly the class this issue exists to remove).
      case "$tok" in
        --body=*|--body-file=*|--subject=*|--author-email=*|--match-head-commit=*|--repo=*|--hostname=*) continue ;;
      esac
      case "$VALUE_FLAGS" in *" $tok "*) expect_value=1; prev_flag="$tok"; continue ;; esac
      case "$BOOL_FLAGS" in *" $tok "*) continue ;; esac
      saw_unknown_flag="$tok"
      continue ;;
  esac
  if [ "$seen_cmd" -eq 0 ]; then CMD="$tok"; seen_cmd=1; continue; fi
  if [ -z "$SUB" ]; then SUB="$tok"; continue; fi
  [ -z "$PR_NUM" ] && PR_NUM="$tok"
done

# `gh api …/merge` is the other shape that lands a merge. The endpoint may carry a
# QUERY or FRAGMENT (`…/merge?s=1`, accepted by gh and still routed to the merge
# endpoint), so strip those before matching — matching the bare token missed it in
# BOTH layers (VGATE).
if [ "$CMD" = "api" ]; then
  api_graphql=0
  for tok in "$@"; do
    [ "$tok" = "graphql" ] && api_graphql=1
  done
  for tok in "$@"; do
    api_path="${tok%%\?*}"
    api_path="${api_path%%#*}"
    # CASE-INSENSITIVELY: GitHub's route matching is case-insensitive, so
    # `PUT …/pulls/1/MERGE` reaches the same endpoint (live probe: 401, where a bogus
    # path is 404) — the case-sensitive glob let it through in both layers (VGATE).
    # `tr` rather than `${x,,}`: macOS ships bash 3.2, which lacks it.
    api_path="$(printf '%s' "$api_path" | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')"
    case "$api_path" in */merge|*/merge/*) IS_ADMIN=1 ;; esac
    # GraphQL. `mergePullRequest` IS the mutation `gh pr merge --admin` itself issues
    # (gh sends no admin field in its REST payload; it is the server-side mutation that
    # performs the bypass), so calling it directly is ADMIN-EQUIVALENT and needs no
    # obfuscation at all (VGATE cycle 2).
    case "$tok" in *mergePullRequest*) IS_ADMIN=1 ;; esac
    # …and when the query itself comes from a FILE or STDIN there is nothing to read, so
    # refuse rather than assume benign (VGATE cycle 3). Gated on `graphql`, so an
    # ordinary REST call that happens to carry an `@` is unaffected.
    if [ "$api_graphql" -eq 1 ]; then
      case "$tok" in
        *@*|--input|--input=*) IS_ADMIN=1 ;;
      esac
    fi
  done
fi

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# THE FLEET COURTESY GATE (#1910)
#
# THE MECHANISM IT ADDRESSES IS NOT THE ONE #1762 ADDRESSED. GitHub's *secondary*
# limit is a CONCURRENCY limit (documented at "no more than 100 concurrent requests",
# shared across REST and GraphQL), and `GET /rate_limit` answers only for the PRIMARY
# budget — it has no field that reports proximity to the secondary limit, and a refusal
# may carry no `retry-after` at all. So `graphql.remaining` reading healthy while every
# GraphQL call is refused is not a contradiction: it is the documented blind spot. That
# is why this gate's health signal is its OWN in-flight count (the `slots/` entries),
# and it never asks the API how healthy it is.
#
# ⛔ AND THE ONE SANCTIONED EXCEPTION, stated here rather than left contradicting: the exempt
# `api rate_limit` read in `discharge_window` IS a read of the API's own health, and it is allowed
# for exactly ONE question and no other — "may this already-recorded window be LIFTED?". It is
# never a readiness probe: it cannot ADMIT a call under a window (it only ever removes one), it
# cannot lift a secondary/concurrency window (`secondary.window`, which `/rate_limit` cannot see at
# all — the blind spot above), and a read that fails, times out or is unreadable KEEPS the window.
# So the invariant above is unchanged in the direction that matters: nothing in this gate concludes
# "the account is healthy, send traffic" from `/rate_limit`. Read this block together with
# `discharge_window`; the earlier wording predates that read, and leaving it standing would make
# this header lie about its own code (#3228 review, finding 8).
#
# Four mechanisms, in the order a call meets them:
#   1. COOLDOWN  — a shared refusal window. A caller that is refused writes a deadline;
#                  every other caller on the box waits it out BEFORE asking, so a burst of
#                  N lanes produces one refusal-and-wait, not N failures.
#   2. SLOTS     — at most MAX_INFLIGHT `gh` processes per machine, admitted under a
#                  mutex and reaped by PID so a dead holder cannot wedge the fleet.
#   3. SPACING   — a minimum interval between call STARTS, under the same mutex, so the
#                  fleet dribbles instead of bursting.
#   4. RETRY     — on a RATE-LIMIT signature in stderr: honour `retry-after`; with none,
#                  wait RETRY_BASE (default 60 s, per GitHub's own guidance) and then
#                  double. Never an immediate identical retry: RETRY_BASE has a floor
#                  of 1 s and the wait is always slept, so the "3/3 attempts, identical"
#                  retry-into-the-burst loop cannot be constructed here.
#
# Portability: macOS ships bash 3.2 — no `${x,,}`, no associative arrays, no `mapfile`.
# `stat`: GNU first, BSD second (GNU `stat -f` prints a filesystem listing to stdout
# before exiting 1, so a BSD-first `||` ladder is the bug itself).
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Milliseconds since the epoch. EPOCHREALTIME (bash >= 5) is the fast path; perl ships
# with macOS and Linux; a whole-second `date` is the last resort (the gate still works,
# at 1 s granularity — never a crash, and never a silent zero).
now_ms() {
  local t
  if [ -n "${EPOCHREALTIME:-}" ]; then
    t="${EPOCHREALTIME//[.,]/}"
    case "$t" in
      ''|*[!0-9]*) ;;
      *) printf '%s' $(( t / 1000 )); return 0 ;;
    esac
  fi
  if t="$(perl -MTime::HiRes=time -e 'printf "%d", time*1000' 2>/dev/null)" && [ -n "$t" ]; then
    case "$t" in
      *[!0-9]*) ;;
      *) printf '%s' "$t"; return 0 ;;
    esac
  fi
  t="$(date +%s 2>/dev/null)"
  case "$t" in ''|*[!0-9]*) t=0 ;; esac
  printf '%s' $(( t * 1000 ))
}

# Fractional sleep (BSD and GNU `sleep` both accept it). Non-positive or unreadable
# values are a no-op, never a usage error.
sleep_ms() {
  local ms="$1" s r
  case "$ms" in ''|*[!0-9]*) return 0 ;; esac
  [ "$ms" -gt 0 ] || return 0
  s=$(( ms / 1000 )); r=$(( ms % 1000 ))
  if [ "$r" -eq 0 ]; then
    sleep "$s"
  else
    # Pad to three digits: `sleep 0.$r` with r=50 is HALF A SECOND, not 50 ms — the
    # difference between a 50 ms admission spin and a 500 ms one, times every lane.
    while [ "${#r}" -lt 3 ]; do r="0$r"; done
    sleep "$s.$r"
  fi
  return 0
}

# An integer setting, or its default. A non-numeric value, and a value of 10+ digits
# (which `[` cannot hold: it errors, and an errored test reads FALSE — i.e. it would
# OPEN the gate it configures), both fall back to the default.
num_or() {
  local v="$1" def="$2"
  case "$v" in ''|*[!0-9]*) printf '%s' "$def"; return 0 ;; esac
  [ "${#v}" -le 9 ] || { printf '%s' "$def"; return 0; }
  printf '%s' "$v"
}

# mtime in ms. GNU stat first, BSD second (see the portability note above).
mtime_ms() {
  local p="$1" s
  s="$(stat -c %Y "$p" 2>/dev/null)"
  case "$s" in ''|*[!0-9]*) s="$(stat -f %m "$p" 2>/dev/null)" ;; esac
  case "$s" in ''|*[!0-9]*) return 1 ;; esac
  printf '%s' $(( s * 1000 ))
}

# ⛔ A PACING NOTE NEVER GOES TO THE CALLER'S STDERR (see STATED LIMITS). It is appended to
# `$GATE_DIR/events.log` always, and buffered to `$NOTEF` for replay only if this call FAILS.
# `gh-rest/gh` does `run_real … 2>&1 | jq`, so a note beside a good answer is a JSONDecodeError,
# and beside a performed `pr merge` it is a reported failure for a merge that happened.
gate_note() {
  # ⛔ REDIRECT ORDER IS THE POINT: `>> file 2>/dev/null` sends a FAILED REDIRECTION to the
  # caller's stderr (bash reports it before the command runs), so an unwritable notes/log path
  # would contaminate a successful call's stderr by another route. `2>/dev/null >>` suppresses it.
  [ -n "${NOTEF:-}" ] && printf 'gh-gate: %s\n' "$*" 2>/dev/null >> "$NOTEF"
  # A DEGRADED GATE MUST STILL LEAVE A TRACE. When the gate dir is unusable the note says the call
  # runs "UNGATED (announced)" — and the buffered copy is discarded on success, so nothing was
  # recorded anywhere and the runbook's "read the log" had nothing to read. Fall back to a
  # durable per-user path outside the gate dir.
  if [ -n "${GATE_DIR:-}" ] && [ -d "$GATE_DIR" ]; then
    printf '%s gh-gate: %s\n' "$(date -u +%s 2>/dev/null)" "$*" 2>/dev/null >> "$GATE_DIR/events.log"
  else
    # ⛔ OWNED DIRS ONLY. The degraded trace must not be a predictable name in a world-writable
    # directory: `>>` follows symlinks, so on a shared /tmp another user could pre-create
    # `/tmp/gh-gate-degraded.<uid>` as a symlink and choose the file we append to. Prefer the
    # user's own tree (the same base gate_init uses for the default gate dir).
    # ⛔ NEVER CREATE THE DEGRADED TRACE'S DIRECTORY AHEAD OF NEED — but do not silently lose it
    # either: `~/.pi/agent` is absent on a fresh clone, and the append then wrote nothing at all.
    mkdir -p "${HOME:-${TMPDIR:-/tmp}}/.pi/agent" 2>/dev/null
    printf '%s gh-gate: %s\n' "$(date -u +%s 2>/dev/null)" "$*" 2>/dev/null \
      >> "${HOME:-${TMPDIR:-/tmp}}/.pi/agent/gh-gate-degraded.log"
  fi
  return 0
}

# Flush the buffered notes to stderr — ONLY when the call is failing. `$1` is the call's rc;
# a zero rc leaves stderr byte-identical to the child's own. The buffer is CONSUMED, so the
# EXIT trap can call this again without printing twice.
flush_notes() {
  # rc=0 → do NOT touch the buffer: `flush_notes "$rc"` then the EXIT trap calls it again, and a
  # truncating write there RECREATED the file the normal path had just removed (litter in the
  # operator's TMPDIR whenever the gate ran degraded).
  [ "${1:-0}" -eq 0 ] && return 0
  if [ -n "${NOTEF:-}" ] && [ -s "$NOTEF" ]; then
    cat "$NOTEF" >&2 2>/dev/null
    : 2>/dev/null > "$NOTEF"
  fi
  return 0
}

cleanup_workdir() {
  # ⛔ GUARDED FOR THE EXIT TRAP: `on_exit` may run after run_paced's locals are gone, and under
  # `set -u` an unguarded "$errf" would abort the cleanup it exists to perform.
  [ -n "${NOTEF:-}" ] && rm -f "$NOTEF" 2>/dev/null
  [ -n "${errf:-}" ] && rm -f "$errf" 2>/dev/null
  [ -n "${outf:-}" ] && rm -f "$outf" 2>/dev/null
  [ "${WORKDIR_OWNED:-0}" -eq 1 ] && [ -n "${WORKDIR:-}" ] && rm -rf "$WORKDIR" 2>/dev/null
  return 0
}

# A caller that declared its OWN per-call bound must not be slept inside this gate:
# `gh-limit.py` sets GH_LIMIT_NO_WAIT precisely so a 60-420 s heartbeat tool gets the refusal
# back at once and reports RATE-LIMITED rather than STALL.
no_wait_wanted() {
  [ "${GH_LIMIT_NO_WAIT:-0}" = "1" ] && return 0
  [ "${AGENT_GH_GATE_NO_WAIT:-0}" = "1" ] && return 0
  return 1
}

# Forward a caller's cancellation to the child we are waiting on, so a killed caller does not
# leave `gh` running through its retries against a bucket GitHub has already refused.
# ⛔ AND THEN STOP. `exit 143` is load-bearing: a trapped TERM interrupts the retry `sleep`,
# and without an exit the loop simply retries — so a cancelled caller would issue the very
# second attempt it was killed to prevent. The EXIT trap releases the slot and flushes notes.
CHILD_PID=""
on_signal() {
  [ -n "${CHILD_PID:-}" ] && kill -TERM "$CHILD_PID" 2>/dev/null
  exit 143
}
on_exit() {
  local r=$?
  release_slot
  flush_notes "$r"
  cleanup_workdir
  return 0
}

# Move an abandoned mutex aside, verifying it is still the one we judged abandoned.
# The verify-after-move is what keeps a steal from discarding a lock a third process
# legitimately re-acquired in the window between our `cat` and our `mv`.
reap_lock() {
  local lock="$1" want="$2" tomb="$lock.reap.$$" got=""
  mv "$lock" "$tomb" 2>/dev/null || return 0
  got="$(cat "$tomb/pid" 2>/dev/null)"
  if [ "$got" != "$want" ]; then
    # Judge a re-acquire by the LOCK, not by the tomb: `mv dir1 dir2` with dir2 an existing
    # DIRECTORY moves the tomb INSIDE the new holder's lock, leaving junk in it and hiding the
    # fact that the steal raced. If the lock is back, our tomb is simply dropped.
    if [ -e "$lock" ]; then rm -rf "$tomb" 2>/dev/null; else mv "$tomb" "$lock" 2>/dev/null; fi
    return 0
  fi
  rm -rf "$tomb" 2>/dev/null
  return 0
}

# The gate's own mutex. `mkdir` is atomic on every filesystem we run on, which is what
# makes slot admission exact rather than approximate.
lock_acquire() {
  local lock="$1" deadline now pid stale
  deadline=$(( $(now_ms) + LOCK_TIMEOUT_MS ))
  while :; do
    if mkdir "$lock" 2>/dev/null; then
      # An UNRECORDED holder is not a holder: if the pid cannot be written, a later reader
      # cannot tell us from a dead lock, and two processes could sit in the critical section
      # at once. Give it back and report the mutex unavailable (the caller fails OPEN, loudly).
      if ! printf '%s\n' "$$" 2>/dev/null > "$lock/pid"; then
        rm -rf "$lock" 2>/dev/null
        return 1
      fi
      return 0
    fi
    now="$(now_ms)"
    pid="$(cat "$lock/pid" 2>/dev/null)"
    case "$pid" in ''|*[!0-9]*) pid="" ;; esac
    if [ -n "$pid" ]; then
      if ! kill -0 "$pid" 2>/dev/null; then
        reap_lock "$lock" "$pid"
        continue
      fi
    else
      # No readable pid: the holder may be in the mkdir→write window (microseconds) or
      # may be gone. Only a lock that is clearly older than that window is touched.
      stale="$(mtime_ms "$lock")" || stale=""
      if [ -n "$stale" ] && [ "$now" -ge $(( stale + 5000 )) ]; then
        reap_lock "$lock" ""
        continue
      fi
    fi
    [ "$now" -ge "$deadline" ] && return 1
    sleep_ms 20
  done
}

lock_release() { rm -rf "$1" 2>/dev/null; return 0; }

# Read the shared refusal window. Files are named `cooldown.<deadline_ms>.<pid>` so the
# window is MONOTONIC under concurrent writers without a lock: a later refusal adds a
# LATER deadline, and it can never be shortened by a writer holding a smaller one.
# Expired files are removed by whoever reads next, so the directory self-cleans.
cooldown_remaining_ms() {
  local now f v max=0
  now="$(now_ms)"
  for f in "$GATE_DIR"/cooldown.*; do
    [ -e "$f" ] || continue
    v="$(cat "$f" 2>/dev/null)"
    case "$v" in ''|*[!0-9]*) rm -f "$f" 2>/dev/null; continue ;; esac
    # A POISONED DEADLINE MUST SELF-HEAL — but against a FIXED ceiling, never the reading lane's
    # own AGENT_GH_GATE_RETRY_MAX: that knob is per-invocation, so a lane that set it lower would
    # delete a window the whole box is legitimately waiting on.
    if [ "$v" -gt $(( now + COOLDOWN_ABS_MAX_S * 1000 )) ]; then rm -f "$f" 2>/dev/null; continue; fi
    if [ "$v" -le "$now" ]; then rm -f "$f" 2>/dev/null; continue; fi
    [ "$v" -gt "$max" ] && max="$v"
  done
  [ "$max" -gt "$now" ] || return 1
  printf '%s' $(( max - now ))
  return 0
}

set_cooldown_ms() {
  local ms="$1" until
  case "$ms" in ''|*[!0-9]*) return 0 ;; esac
  [ "$ms" -gt 0 ] || return 0
  until=$(( $(now_ms) + ms ))
  printf '%s\n' "$until" 2>/dev/null > "$GATE_DIR/cooldown.$until.$$"
  # The deadline is also the caller's answer: `secondary.window` records the window it protects.
  printf '%s' "$until"
  return 0
}

# ── A SHARED WINDOW IS EVIDENCE WITH A WINDOW, NOT A STATE ────────────────────────────────────
# ⛔ THIS LAYER USED TO SLEEP ON A COOLDOWN IT NEVER RE-DERIVED, AND THAT IS THE FLEET STALL.
# MEASURED 2026-11-01, this box, 19:34-19:38 UTC: `gh run view` was refused
# `HTTP 403: API rate limit exceeded for user ID 81560491` three times. Each refusal wrote a
# BOX-WIDE 60 s then 120 s window, so every lane's every call — including reads of a completely
# different endpoint — queued behind it, while the SAME account's documented-exempt
# `GET /rate_limit` read reported core 4,684/5,000, graphql 4,976/5,000, and every other bucket at
# full availability. No bucket was exhausted; the window was not derivable from the evidence.
# COST OF THE FALSE WINDOW, measured the same minute: `gh api rate_limit` through this shim TIMED
# OUT at 90 s, while the identical read took 0.432 s direct over HTTP and 0.379 s via the real
# binary. The limit was OURS, not GitHub's.
# gh-limit.py states the rule this layer was breaking, in its own docstring: "it REVALIDATES with
# one exempt REST `rate_limit` read before any long wait". This is that read.
#
# ⛔ IT CAN NEVER DISCHARGE A SECONDARY LIMIT. Secondary/abuse refusals are INVISIBLE to
# `/rate_limit` (gh-limit.py: "the primary counter does not predict a secondary limit"), so a
# healthy primary read neither corroborates nor contradicts one. `$GATE_DIR/secondary.window`
# records that the MOST RECENT window was written by such a refusal, and while it exists the window
# is honoured unconditionally. Absence of evidence is never a discharge: a revalidation that cannot
# complete (no real binary, unparseable body, timeout) leaves the window exactly as it was.
#
# ⛔ AND IT CAN NEVER DISCHARGE A `retry-after` WINDOW EITHER — `$GATE_DIR/retry-after.window`. That
# deadline is the SERVER's own instruction, and headroom on the primary counter does not contradict
# it; gh-limit.py refuses the identical discharge and states the measured consequence: "Clearing a
# 300 s cool-down on `remaining > 0` put every later client on the box back inside a cool-down this
# layer was still obeying" (`gh-limit.py:843-851`, `kind != "retry-after"`). The header is also the
# documented way a secondary refusal announces itself when it does not use the runbook's exact
# wording (`docs/ops/gh-rate-limits.md` — secondary "may carry `retry-after`, may not"), so folding
# it in here closes both ways that window could otherwise be discharged by a read that cannot see it.
#
# ⛔ ONE EXEMPT READ AT A TIME, BOX-WIDE. This read fires ONLY when a shared window is open — the
# contention peak — so N blocked lanes would otherwise issue N concurrent requests, and the gate's
# health signal is its OWN in-flight count (#1910). gh-limit.py:2941 takes a single-flight lock for
# the identical read, calling it "the ONE gh request the layer issues unserialised … One invariant,
# no exemptions". `single_flight_acquire` is that lock here: a second lane honours the window
# instead of asking again, so the fleet never converts a primary window into a concurrency burst.

# ⛔ EVERY BUCKET, NOT JUST `core`. This used to read `resources.core.remaining` alone and then
# claim "every bucket available" in the note. A `pr`/`issue`/`search` refusal is raised on
# `graphql` or `search`, which `/rate_limit` reports as SEPARATE buckets, so a healthy `core` with an
# exhausted `graphql` discharged a window that was entirely justified — and the operator's own log
# asserted the opposite of what was read (#3228 review, finding 3).
#
# Prints one line and never invents a verdict:
#   `OK lowest=<bucket> <rem>/<lim>`   no bucket is low  → the window may be discharged
#   `LOW <bucket> <rem>/<lim>`         one IS low        → the window is CREDIBLE, honour it
#   (nothing; rc=1)                    unreadable body   → honour it
#
# ⛔ TWO THRESHOLDS, because one absolute floor is wrong for the small buckets: `search` caps at 30,
# so "remaining <= 100" would call EVERY healthy search bucket low and make this gate permanently
# inert — the failure mode that looks exactly like a working fix. A bucket is low when it is at or
# below EITHER the absolute floor (and big enough for that floor to mean anything) OR 10% of its own
# limit. Both directions are pinned in `tests/gh-shim/run.sh`, so neither an inert gate nor a false
# discharge can pass.
# Python is already a hard dependency of the layer above (`gh-limit.py`); without it this fails and
# the window is kept, which is the safe direction.
rate_limit_verdict() {
  local f="$1" out
  [ -s "$f" ] || return 1
  # ⛔ FLUSH LEFT, EVERY LINE. `python3 -c` receives this text VERBATIM — a single leading space
  # on line 2 is an IndentationError ("unexpected indent"), which this function would report as
  # "unreadable body", i.e. a revalidation that can NEVER discharge a window and looks like it is
  # working. MEASURED while proving this change: with the indent, a 110 s window was slept out in
  # full (112.9 s) and the log said "could not re-derive … (unreadable body)".
  out="$(python3 -c 'import json,sys
floor_n=int(sys.argv[2])
d=json.load(open(sys.argv[1]))
res=d.get("resources")
if not isinstance(res,dict) or not res: sys.exit(1)
low=None
lowest=None
for name,b in res.items():
    if not isinstance(b,dict): continue
    rem=b.get("remaining")
    lim=b.get("limit")
    if not isinstance(rem,int) or not isinstance(lim,int): continue
    if lim<=0: continue
    if rem<=0 or rem*100<=lim*10 or (lim>=floor_n and rem<=floor_n):
        if low is None: low=(name,rem,lim)
    if lowest is None or rem*1000000//lim < lowest[1]*1000000//lowest[2]:
        lowest=(name,rem,lim)
if lowest is None: sys.exit(1)
if low is not None:
    print("LOW %s %d/%d" % low)
else:
    print("OK lowest=%s %d/%d" % lowest)' "$f" "$REVALIDATE_CORE_FLOOR" 2>/dev/null)" || return 1
  case "$out" in
    'OK lowest='?*) printf '%s' "$out"; return 0 ;;
    'LOW '?*) return 2 ;;
  esac
  return 1
}

# Run a command with a hard millisecond bound. macOS has no `timeout(1)`, and this shim must not
# depend on one: background + a poll + TERM-then-KILL is the portable shape.
run_bounded() {
  local bound="$1" outf="$2" pid spent=0; shift 2
  "$@" >"$outf" 2>/dev/null &
  pid=$!
  # ⛔ THE REVALIDATION CHILD IS A CHILD TOO. `on_signal` forwards the caller's TERM to `CHILD_PID`
  # only, so without this the exempt read survives the shim it belongs to — re-parented to init and
  # still holding a request against a bucket we were told not to touch (#3228 review, finding 5).
  CHILD_PID="$pid"
  while [ "$spent" -lt "$bound" ]; do
    kill -0 "$pid" 2>/dev/null || { wait "$pid" 2>/dev/null; return $?; }
    sleep_ms 100
    spent=$(( spent + 100 ))
  done
  kill -TERM "$pid" 2>/dev/null
  sleep_ms 100
  kill -KILL "$pid" 2>/dev/null
  wait "$pid" 2>/dev/null
  CHILD_PID=""
  return 124
}

# One window-KIND flag: a deadline recorded by the refusal that wrote the window, honoured until
# TIME passes it and by nothing else.
# ⛔ THE FLAG CARRIES ITS DEADLINE. An empty flag is not a fact — but neither is a box-wide
# "secondary" boolean: `cooldown_remaining_ms` is a MONOTONIC MAX across files, so a secondary
# window's 120 s deadline outlives the 60 s one a later ordinary refusal writes, and a boolean that
# any ordinary refusal clears would un-protect it (#3228 review, finding 2 — measured: lane B
# refused without a secondary phrase, flag gone, the 120 s secondary window left dischargeable by
# lane C). So the flag records the deadline it protects and is only ever cleared BY TIME.
window_kind_live() {
  local f="$GATE_DIR/$1" v now
  [ -e "$f" ] || return 1
  v="$(cat "$f" 2>/dev/null)"
  now="$(now_ms)"
  case "$v" in
    ''|*[!0-9]*)
      # No deadline recorded (an older writer, or one killed mid-write). Absence of evidence is
      # never a discharge, so it is HONOURED — but it is pruned once no window is left to protect,
      # so a writer that died cannot wedge discharge off forever.
      cooldown_remaining_ms >/dev/null || rm -f "$f" 2>/dev/null
      return 0 ;;
  esac
  [ "$v" -gt "$now" ] && return 0
  # Its window is gone: it protected nothing, and it must not shadow a LATER primary window.
  rm -f "$f" 2>/dev/null
  return 1
}
# A window a SECONDARY-shaped refusal wrote: `/rate_limit` cannot see it at all (#1762).
secondary_live() { window_kind_live secondary.window; }
# A window the SERVER told us to wait out: headroom does not contradict it (gh-limit.py, #1762).
retry_after_live() { window_kind_live retry-after.window; }

# Non-blocking single-flight for the exempt read (see the header block): at most ONE such read per
# box, never a queue behind it. A lane that cannot take it HONOURS the window rather than issue a
# second request at the contention peak. A dead holder is reaped by its recorded pid, so a killed
# shim cannot wedge revalidation off forever.
single_flight_acquire() {
  local lock="$1" pid stale now
  if ! mkdir "$lock" 2>/dev/null; then
    pid="$(cat "$lock/pid" 2>/dev/null)"
    case "$pid" in ''|*[!0-9]*) pid="" ;; esac
    if [ -n "$pid" ]; then
      kill -0 "$pid" 2>/dev/null && return 1
    else
      # Unrecorded holder: it may be in the mkdir→write window (microseconds) or gone, so only a
      # lock clearly older than that window is touched.
      stale="$(mtime_ms "$lock")" || stale=""
      now="$(now_ms)"
      [ -n "$stale" ] && [ "$now" -lt $(( stale + 5000 )) ] && return 1
    fi
    rm -rf "$lock" 2>/dev/null
    mkdir "$lock" 2>/dev/null || return 1
  fi
  if ! printf '%s\n' "$$" 2>/dev/null > "$lock/pid"; then
    rm -rf "$lock" 2>/dev/null
    return 1
  fi
  return 0
}

# Re-derive an open shared window against the exempt read. 0 = discharged (proceed now),
# 1 = keep the window. It only ever REMOVES a window on positive evidence that the account is fine.
discharge_window() {
  local outf rem vrd minrem rlock
  [ "$REVALIDATE_ON" -eq 1 ] || return 1
  # A window a SECONDARY-shaped refusal wrote is never discharged by a primary read, and neither is
  # one the server itself instructed (see `window_kind_live`).
  secondary_live && return 1
  retry_after_live && return 1
  rem="$(cooldown_remaining_ms)" || return 1
  # Only bother once the wait is long enough to be worth 0.4 s of read (the measured cost).
  [ "$rem" -ge "$REVALIDATE_MIN_MS" ] || return 1
  rlock="$GATE_DIR/revalidate.lock"
  if ! single_flight_acquire "$rlock"; then
    gate_note "another lane is already re-deriving the shared window — honouring it instead of issuing a second exempt read"
    return 1
  fi
  outf="$WORKDIR/revalidate.$$"
  if ! run_bounded "$REVALIDATE_TIMEOUT_MS" "$outf" "$REAL" api rate_limit; then
    rm -rf "$rlock" 2>/dev/null
    rm -f "$outf" 2>/dev/null
    gate_note "could not re-derive the shared rate-limit window (the exempt /rate_limit read failed or exceeded ${REVALIDATE_TIMEOUT_MS}ms) — honouring it"
    return 1
  fi
  rm -rf "$rlock" 2>/dev/null
  rate_limit_verdict "$outf"; vrd=$?
  minrem="$(sed -n 's/^OK lowest=[^ ]* //p' "$outf" 2>/dev/null)"
  [ -n "$minrem" ] || minrem="(unreadable)"
  case "$vrd" in
    0) ;;  # every bucket has headroom
    1)
      rm -f "$outf" 2>/dev/null
      gate_note "could not re-derive the shared gh throttle window (unreadable quota body) — honouring it"
      return 1 ;;
    *)
      # ⛔ THE NOTE STATES EXACTLY WHAT WAS READ. The old wording said "core remaining=…" and, on
      # the discharge path, "every bucket available" — a claim about buckets the code never read, in
      # the one log an operator debugs with (#3228 review, finding 3).
      gate_note "the shared gh throttle window is CORROBORATED by the exempt read (a bucket is low: $(sed -n 's/^LOW //p' "$outf" 2>/dev/null)) — honouring it"
      rm -f "$outf" 2>/dev/null
      return 1 ;;
  esac
  rm -f "$outf" 2>/dev/null
  # Positive evidence the account is fine: the window cannot be explained by a primary limit.
  rm -f "$GATE_DIR"/cooldown.* 2>/dev/null
  gate_note "stale shared gh throttle window DISCHARGED (exempt read: no bucket low; lowest remaining $minrem) — this call proceeds now"
  return 0
}

# Sleep out the shared window, in <=60 s slices so a lane that extends the window while we wait is
# still honoured — but under ONE budget, and re-derived before the first sleep.
# Returns 0 = no window left (proceed), 3 = the budget was spent while a window was still open.
# ⛔ ONE BUDGET, NOT TWO MULTIPLIED. This wait sits BELOW a retry arm that sleeps 60 s + 120 s of its
# own and BELOW `gh-limit.py`'s 4 attempts, so an unbounded wait here is a ceiling that outlives its
# derivation: the caller's timeout is spent inside a layer that never counts it. Past the budget the
# call stops waiting and says so, instead of hanging into the caller's timeout (which reads, to the
# lane above, exactly like "GitHub is slow").
wait_for_cooldown() {
  # ⛔ THE BUDGET IS PER gh CALL, NOT PER ATTEMPT. `run_paced` re-enters this on every pass of its
  # retry loop, so a per-call `local spent=0` let ONE gh call spend MAX_ATTEMPTS budgets — 240 s of
  # waits here plus the retry arm's own 60 s + 120 s, under gh-limit.py's 4 attempts (#3228 review,
  # finding 6: "a ceiling that outlives its derivation", one layer up). WAIT_SPENT_MS carries it.
  local rem chunk announced=0 spent="${WAIT_SPENT_MS:-0}"
  while rem="$(cooldown_remaining_ms)"; do
    case "$rem" in ''|*[!0-9]*) break ;; esac
    [ "$rem" -gt 0 ] || break
    if [ "$announced" -eq 0 ]; then
      if discharge_window; then break; fi
      gate_note "the account is in a shared rate-limit window — waiting $(( (rem + 999) / 1000 ))s before this call (budget ${WAIT_BUDGET_MS}ms)"
      announced=1
    fi
    if [ "$spent" -ge "$WAIT_BUDGET_MS" ]; then WAIT_SPENT_MS="$spent"; return 3; fi
    chunk="$rem"
    [ "$chunk" -gt 60000 ] && chunk=60000
    # Never sleep past the budget: a 120 s slice against a 30 s budget is the same hang.
    if [ $(( spent + chunk )) -gt "$WAIT_BUDGET_MS" ]; then chunk=$(( WAIT_BUDGET_MS - spent )); fi
    [ "$chunk" -gt 0 ] || { WAIT_SPENT_MS="$spent"; return 3; }
    sleep_ms "$chunk"
    spent=$(( spent + chunk ))
  done
  WAIT_SPENT_MS="$spent"
  # A window that outlived the budget is a CONTENDED verdict, not an empty answer.
  if cooldown_remaining_ms >/dev/null; then return 3; fi
  return 0
}

# Admit one call: 0 = holding a slot (MY_SLOT), 1 = waited out ACQUIRE_TIMEOUT, 2 = the
# mutex itself was unavailable. Both failures are announced by the caller and the call
# proceeds UNGATED: an unbounded wait would hang every lane on a wedged lock — the gate
# is a courtesy, so its own failure mode must not be a fleet stall. A dead holder is
# reaped by PID, which is why the timeout is nearly unreachable in practice.
admit_slot() {
  local deadline now n f pid
  deadline=$(( $(now_ms) + ACQUIRE_TIMEOUT_MS ))
  while :; do
    if lock_acquire "$GATE_DIR/admit.lock"; then
      n=0
      for f in "$GATE_DIR"/slots/*; do
        [ -e "$f" ] || continue
        pid="$(cat "$f" 2>/dev/null)"
        case "$pid" in
          ''|*[!0-9]*) rm -f "$f" 2>/dev/null; continue ;;
        esac
        if ! kill -0 "$pid" 2>/dev/null; then rm -f "$f" 2>/dev/null; continue; fi
        n=$(( n + 1 ))
      done
      if [ "$n" -lt "$MAX_INFLIGHT" ]; then
        MY_SLOT="$GATE_DIR/slots/$$.$(now_ms).$RANDOM"
        if printf '%s\n' "$$" 2>/dev/null > "$MY_SLOT"; then
          lock_release "$GATE_DIR/admit.lock"
          return 0
        fi
        MY_SLOT=""
      fi
      lock_release "$GATE_DIR/admit.lock"
      now="$(now_ms)"
      [ "$now" -ge "$deadline" ] && return 1
      sleep_ms 50
      continue
    fi
    # A mutex we cannot take is not worth waiting ACQUIRE_TIMEOUT for: `mkdir` being REFUSED
    # does not become possible with time, and `lock_acquire` has ALREADY spent LOCK_TIMEOUT_MS
    # trying. Re-looping to the admission deadline turned a courtesy gate into a 299 s in-shim
    # block (measured, default knobs) that consumed the bounded caller's own 60-420 s budget and
    # produced the very STALL that `GH_LIMIT_NO_WAIT` exists to avoid. Fail open NOW.
    return 2
  done
}

release_slot() {
  [ -n "${MY_SLOT:-}" ] || return 0
  rm -f "$MY_SLOT" 2>/dev/null
  MY_SLOT=""
  return 0
}

# Space call STARTS. The mutex is held ACROSS the sleep on purpose: that is what turns
# "each caller sleeps a bit" into a total order, so N callers space out N*interval apart
# rather than all waking at the same instant.
# ⛔ BOUNDED BY `LOCK_TIMEOUT_MS`, NOT BY THE SLOT CAP. NESTED calls skip admit_slot by design,
# and a call whose admission timed out runs ungated — so more than MAX_INFLIGHT callers CAN be
# here at once, and at ~9 nested callers each holding the mutex for INTERVAL_MS the wait exceeds
# LOCK_TIMEOUT_MS and the tick is skipped (announced, fail-open). Keep LOCK_TIMEOUT_MS above
# MAX_INFLIGHT x INTERVAL_MS if you want the spacing to hold under nesting.
pace_tick() {
  [ "$INTERVAL_MS" -gt 0 ] || return 0
  local last now wait_ms
  if ! lock_acquire "$GATE_DIR/tick.lock"; then
    gate_note "the pacing mutex is unavailable — this call is not spaced (the slot cap still holds)"
    return 0
  fi
  last="$(cat "$GATE_DIR/last" 2>/dev/null)"
  case "$last" in ''|*[!0-9]*) last=0 ;; esac
  now="$(now_ms)"
  wait_ms=$(( last + INTERVAL_MS - now ))
  # A clock that jumped BACKWARD must not turn into an unbounded sleep.
  [ "$wait_ms" -gt "$INTERVAL_MS" ] && wait_ms="$INTERVAL_MS"
  if [ "$wait_ms" -gt 0 ]; then sleep_ms "$wait_ms"; now="$(now_ms)"; fi
  printf '%s\n' "$now" 2>/dev/null > "$GATE_DIR/last.$$"
  mv -f "$GATE_DIR/last.$$" "$GATE_DIR/last" 2>/dev/null
  lock_release "$GATE_DIR/tick.lock"
  return 0
}

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# THE TRANSPORT RETRY (#2148) — a dropped connection is not a decision.
#
# MEASURED (2026-10-24). cell-3 lane `mainredai`: `[http-pool-hygiene] rotated connection pool
# (message_end: Connection error.; rotation #3)`, then `Connection error.`, then the reflect hook
# exited rc=1 — the lane's work was lost. Lane `confA2` died the same way two bursts later. cell-1,
# three IDENTICAL `gh api rate_limit` calls two seconds apart: `ok 704ms remaining=2431`, `ok 891ms
# remaining=2419`, `FAIL error connecting to api.github.com`. Quota was healthy, so the fault is
# TRANSPORT, and cell-3's next four calls all succeeded — intermittent per call, which a single-shot
# `gh` cannot survive and a retry can.
#
# WHAT IS NEVER RETRIED HERE:
#   * a response GitHub produced — any HTTP 4xx/5xx, `422`, `Not Found`, a usage error. Retrying a
#     422 turns one honest refusal into three; retrying a 4xx is how a real error is mistaken for
#     flake.
#   * a RATE-LIMIT refusal — that is a DECISION the pacing gate above already owns, with its own
#     wait and its own shared cooldown. Retrying it here would hammer through the ceiling that gate
#     exists to hold, so the transport arm refuses on the same signatures `rate_limited` matches.
#   * any command that might MUTATE — see the STATED LIMITS bullet; zero retries by default.
TRANSPORT_ATTEMPTS=3   # total attempts, NOT retries: retries 1 and 2, then the caller's rc

# Is this stderr a TRANSPORT failure? Prints the matched reason (the parenthetical of the log line)
# and returns 0 on a match, fails otherwise. A TEXT test for the same reason `rate_limited` is one:
# stderr is all `gh` leaves.
transport_reason() {
  local f="$1" rc="$2" hit
  [ "$rc" -ne 0 ] || return 1
  # ⛔ AN HTTP RESPONSE IS NEVER TRANSPORT, AND THIS IS CHECKED FIRST. If any status reached stderr,
  # GitHub ANSWERED — no transport-looking word inside an error body may reclassify it, and the
  # rate-limit signatures are included here so this arm can never pick up a decision that the gate's
  # own arm owns.
  if [ -s "$f" ] && grep -Eqi 'http[ /:]?[0-9][0-9][0-9]|status code:?[[:space:]]*[0-9][0-9][0-9]|not found|bad credentials|unprocessable|api rate limit|secondary rate limit|abuse detection|retry-after:' "$f" 2>/dev/null; then
    return 1
  fi
  if [ ! -s "$f" ]; then
    # ⛔ THE "EMPTY STDERR" SHAPE IS *NOT* A TRANSPORT FAILURE — MEASURED, AND IT MATTERS. gh exits 1
    # with NO message on purpose (`cmdutil.SilentError`) precisely where the ANSWER is a verdict:
    # `gh pr checks` does it when checks FAILED, `gh run view --exit-status` when the run failed.
    # Both are on this layer's read whitelist and `pr checks` is one of the fleet's hottest reads, so
    # treating rc=1+empty stderr as a blip would re-run EVERY RED PR three times, add 4 s to it and
    # write `transport retry … (no response)` into events.log — a false cause on the surface the
    # runbook tells operators to read. Every transport failure actually measured (2026-10-24) PRINTS
    # (`error connecting to api.github.com`, `Connection error.`, `i/o timeout`), so the text test
    # above is the whole signature and this arm stays closed.
    return 1
  fi
  # `-m1` and NOT `| head -1`: a quiet early-exiting consumer in a pipeline reads as FAILURE under
  # `pipefail` once stderr outgrows the pipe buffer (the #841 class this file refuses elsewhere).
  hit="$(grep -oiE -m1 'error connecting|connection error|connection reset|i/o timeout|tls handshake timeout|no such host|temporary failure in name resolution|unexpected eof' "$f" 2>/dev/null)"
  [ -n "$hit" ] || return 1
  printf '%s' "$hit"
  return 0
}

# ⛔ A TRANSPORT RETRY RE-RUNS THE WHOLE ARGV, SO "MAY THIS CALL WRITE?" IS THE SAFETY QUESTION.
# A WHITELIST, not a blacklist: an unrecognised shape is NOT retried (cost: one flaky-call failure,
# i.e. today's behaviour), whereas a blacklist missing one spelling runs a write twice.
read_only_call() {
  local a w1="" w2="" prev="" method="" body=0
  for a in "$@"; do
    case "$a" in
      --method) prev="method" ;;
      --method=*) method="${a#--method=}" ;;
      -X) prev="method" ;;
      -X?*) method="${a#-X}" ;;
      # ⛔ A FLAG'S VALUE IS NOT A POSITIONAL. It matters most for flags that PRECEDE the
      # subcommand: `gh -R o/r pr view 1` put `o/r` in the w1 slot, so the call read as "not pr" and
      # was silently not retried — a shape the fleet actually issues. These take a value; skip it.
      -H|--header|-q|--jq|-t|--template|--cache|--hostname|-p|--preview|-R|--repo) prev="flagval" ;;
      -f|--field|-F|--raw-field|--input) body=1; prev="flagval" ;;
      -f?*|-F?*|--field=*|--raw-field=*|--input=*) body=1 ;;
      -*) ;;
      *)
        case "$prev" in
          method) method="$a" ;;
          flagval) : ;;
          *)
            if [ -z "$w1" ]; then w1="$a"
            elif [ -z "$w2" ]; then w2="$a"
            fi ;;
        esac
        prev="" ;;
    esac
  done
  case "$w1" in
    api)
      # `graphql` can BE the merge mutation, and a file/stdin body is unclassifiable (#984) — never
      # retried. `-f/-F/--input` imply POST even with no `-X`; with no method at all it is a GET.
      # `graphql` — ANY spelling: `graphql`, `/graphql`, a full `https://api.github.com/graphql?…` URL.
      # A prefix test let the path/URL forms through, contradicting the STATED LIMITS bullet.
      case "$w2" in *graphql*) return 1 ;; esac
      [ "$body" -eq 0 ] || return 1
      case "${method:-GET}" in GET|get|HEAD|head) return 0 ;; esac
      return 1 ;;
    # gh's READ verbs. Anything absent here — `pr merge`, `pr create|comment|ready`, `issue close`,
    # `release create`, `workflow run`, `variable set`, `auth login`, … — is NOT retried.
    pr)       case "$w2" in view|list|checks|diff|status) return 0 ;; esac ;;
    issue)    case "$w2" in view|list) return 0 ;; esac ;;
    run)      case "$w2" in view|list) return 0 ;; esac ;;
    workflow) case "$w2" in view|list) return 0 ;; esac ;;
    release)  case "$w2" in view|list) return 0 ;; esac ;;
    repo)     case "$w2" in view|list) return 0 ;; esac ;;
    label|secret|variable|cache|gist) case "$w2" in list|view) return 0 ;; esac ;;
    search)   return 0 ;;
    auth)     case "$w2" in status) return 0 ;; esac ;;
  esac
  return 1
}

# The retry is VISIBLE — in events.log always, and on stderr ONLY when the call finally fails (the
# ⛔ above `gate_note`: a line beside a good answer is a JSONDecodeError for `gh-rest/gh`, and a
# reported FAILURE for a `pr merge` GitHub already performed).
transport_note() {
  local line="$1"
  [ -n "${NOTEF:-}" ] && printf 'gh-shim: %s\n' "$line" 2>/dev/null >> "$NOTEF"
  # ⛔ REDIRECT ORDER: `>> file` then `2>/dev/null` would send a FAILED redirection to the caller's
  # stderr — the exact contamination this function exists to avoid.
  if [ -n "${GATE_DIR:-}" ] && [ -d "$GATE_DIR" ]; then
    printf '%s gh-shim: %s\n' "$(date -u +%s 2>/dev/null)" "$line" 2>/dev/null >> "$GATE_DIR/events.log"
  else
    mkdir -p "${HOME:-${TMPDIR:-/tmp}}/.pi/agent" 2>/dev/null
    printf '%s gh-shim: %s\n' "$(date -u +%s 2>/dev/null)" "$line" 2>/dev/null \
      >> "${HOME:-${TMPDIR:-/tmp}}/.pi/agent/gh-gate-degraded.log"
  fi
  return 0
}

# Is this stderr a RATE-LIMIT refusal? Deliberately NOT "any 403": a permissions 403 is
# not a burst, and retrying a mutating command on it would run the command twice.
rate_limited() {
  local f="$1"
  [ -s "$f" ] || return 1
  # ⛔ ONE COMMAND, NO PIPELINE. `tr … | grep -Eq …` under `set -o pipefail` returns 141: `grep -q`
  # exits at its first match, `tr` takes SIGPIPE, and the 141 then reads as "not rate limited" —
  # so a refusal whose stderr exceeds the pipe buffer (~16-64 KB: a `--paginate` failure, a
  # replayed error body) would neither retry NOR write the shared cooldown, with no signal.
  # The same construction is forbidden above in find_real_gh for exactly this reason.
  grep -Eqi 'api rate limit|secondary rate limit|abuse detection|http 429|status code:? 429|retry-after:' "$f" 2>/dev/null
}

# The wait before retry number <attempt>, in ms. `retry-after` first (that is the server's
# own instruction); otherwise RETRY_BASE doubled per attempt — GitHub's guidance when the
# header is absent is "wait at least one minute". RETRY_MAX bounds any single wait.
retry_wait_ms() {
  local f="$1" attempt="$2" s base i=1
  # ONE awk, NOT `tr | sed | head`. awk can stop at the first match, so there is no early-exit
  # SIGPIPE to mis-read, and `tolower()` is POSIX (BSD awk has no IGNORECASE).
  s="$(awk '{ l = tolower($0)
              if (match(l, /retry-after:[ \t]*[0-9]+/)) {
                v = substr(l, RSTART, RLENGTH); sub(/retry-after:[ \t]*/, "", v); print v; exit } }' \
        "$f" 2>/dev/null | head -n 1)"
  case "$s" in ''|*[!0-9]*) s="" ;; esac
  # ⛔ A VALUE `[ ]` CANNOT HOLD IS TREATED AS ABSENT, NOT CLAMPED. `[ 10^19 -gt 900 ]` ERRORS,
  # and an errored test reads FALSE — so the clamp would be skipped, the `*1000` would wrap, and
  # `sleep` would reject the result and fall through to an IMMEDIATE identical retry while the
  # wrapped value parked every lane on the box. Same ≤9-digit rule num_or applies to knobs.
  [ -n "$s" ] && { [ "${#s}" -le 9 ] || s=""; }
  if [ -n "$s" ]; then
    base="$s"
  else
    base="$RETRY_BASE_S"
    while [ "$i" -lt "$attempt" ]; do base=$(( base * 2 )); i=$(( i + 1 )); done
  fi
  [ "$base" -lt 1 ] && base=1
  [ "$base" -gt "$RETRY_MAX_S" ] && base="$RETRY_MAX_S"
  printf '%s' $(( base * 1000 ))
  return 0
}

gate_init() {
  local base
  GATE_ON=1; NESTED=0; MY_SLOT=""; WORKDIR=""; WORKDIR_OWNED=0
  base="${HOME:-${TMPDIR:-/tmp}}"
  GATE_DIR="${AGENT_GH_GATE_DIR:-$base/.pi/agent/gh-gate}"
  MAX_INFLIGHT="$(num_or "${AGENT_GH_GATE_MAX_INFLIGHT:-}" 2)"
  INTERVAL_MS="$(num_or "${AGENT_GH_GATE_INTERVAL_MS:-}" 250)"
  MAX_ATTEMPTS="$(num_or "${AGENT_GH_GATE_MAX_ATTEMPTS:-}" 3)"
  RETRY_BASE_S="$(num_or "${AGENT_GH_GATE_RETRY_BASE:-}" 60)"
  RETRY_MAX_S="$(num_or "${AGENT_GH_GATE_RETRY_MAX:-}" 900)"
  # FIXED, not a knob: the self-heal ceiling for the SHARED cooldown. A per-call knob must not
  # decide what counts as an impossible deadline for the whole box (see cooldown_remaining_ms).
  COOLDOWN_ABS_MAX_S=86400
  # ── ONE WAIT BUDGET (see wait_for_cooldown). Seconds, so it reads like the other budgets here.
  WAIT_BUDGET_MS=$(( $(num_or "${AGENT_GH_GATE_WAIT_BUDGET:-}" 120) * 1000 ))
  [ "$WAIT_BUDGET_MS" -ge 0 ] || WAIT_BUDGET_MS=120000
  # Re-derivation knobs. 1 = on. The read bound is FIXED and small: it sits on the failure path of
  # every call on the box, so it must be far below the waits it replaces (measured cost 0.379-0.432 s).
  REVALIDATE_ON="$(num_or "${AGENT_GH_GATE_REVALIDATE:-}" 1)"
  [ "$REVALIDATE_ON" -eq 0 ] || REVALIDATE_ON=1
  REVALIDATE_TIMEOUT_MS=5000
  # A primary bucket this low means the window is credible; above it, the window cannot be explained
  # by a primary limit. Deliberately a FLOOR, not a percentage: 100 requests is ~20 s of fleet reads.
  REVALIDATE_CORE_FLOOR="$(num_or "${AGENT_GH_GATE_REVALIDATE_FLOOR:-}" 100)"
  [ "$REVALIDATE_CORE_FLOOR" -ge 0 ] || REVALIDATE_CORE_FLOOR=100
  # Below this remaining wait, sleeping is cheaper than a 0.4 s network read.
  REVALIDATE_MIN_MS=3000
  ACQUIRE_TIMEOUT_MS=$(( $(num_or "${AGENT_GH_GATE_ACQUIRE_TIMEOUT:-}" 300) * 1000 ))
  LOCK_TIMEOUT_MS="$(num_or "${AGENT_GH_GATE_LOCK_TIMEOUT_MS:-}" 2000)"
  # Coerce EVERY limit before its first arithmetic read: `[ -ge ]` on a non-number is
  # FALSE, and a false test in this chain OPENS the gate it configures.
  [ "$MAX_INFLIGHT" -ge 1 ] || MAX_INFLIGHT=1
  [ "$MAX_ATTEMPTS" -ge 1 ] || MAX_ATTEMPTS=1
  # Floor 1 s, not 0: an immediate identical retry cannot succeed by construction and is
  # exactly the loop #1762's `3/3 attempts, identical` recorded.
  [ "$RETRY_BASE_S" -ge 1 ] || RETRY_BASE_S=60
  [ "$RETRY_MAX_S" -ge "$RETRY_BASE_S" ] || RETRY_MAX_S="$RETRY_BASE_S"
  [ "$LOCK_TIMEOUT_MS" -ge 50 ] || LOCK_TIMEOUT_MS=2000
  [ "$INTERVAL_MS" -ge 0 ] || INTERVAL_MS=0
  # A private directory: the slot count is the gate's ONLY health signal, so another user
  # must not be able to add to it (nor read who is calling what).
  if ! mkdir -p "$GATE_DIR/slots" 2>/dev/null; then GATE_ON=0; fi
  chmod 700 "$GATE_DIR" "$GATE_DIR/slots" 2>/dev/null
  if [ "$GATE_ON" -eq 1 ]; then
    WORKDIR="$GATE_DIR"
  elif WORKDIR="$(mktemp -d "${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/gh-gate.XXXXXX" 2>/dev/null)" && [ -n "$WORKDIR" ]; then
    # ⛔ OWNED MEANS WE CREATED IT AND MAY DELETE IT. Never inferred from "not equal to
    # GATE_DIR": the fallback below is the operator's TMPDIR, and the cleanup at the end of
    # run_paced is `rm -rf "$WORKDIR"` — so the old test DELETED $TMPDIR WITH ITS CONTENTS
    # whenever mktemp failed (reproduced: a directory holding a file was silently removed by a
    # call that then exited 0, with nothing printed anywhere). Silent destruction of work.
    WORKDIR_OWNED=1
  fi
  [ -n "$WORKDIR" ] || WORKDIR="${TMPDIR:-/tmp}"
  # A NESTED call (the evidence verifier shells out to `gh`) already runs inside the
  # parent's slot and must not take a second one — with a cap of 2, two parents each
  # nesting once would deadlock. It still honours the cooldown and the spacing.
  if [ -n "${AGENT_GH_GATE_SLOT:-}" ] && [ -d "${AGENT_GH_GATE_SLOT}" ]; then NESTED=1; fi
  return 0
}

# Run the real gh behind the gate. Replaces the bare `exec` at both call sites: a bare
# exec cannot inspect stderr, so it can neither honour `retry-after` nor tell a burst
# refusal from a real failure.
run_paced() {
  local rc=0 tries=0 ttries=0 treason="" rem wait_ms interactive=0 errf="" outf="" adm=0 rc_wait=0
  gate_init
  NOTEF="$WORKDIR/notes.$$"
  errf="$WORKDIR/err.$$"
  outf="$WORKDIR/out.$$"
  CHILD_PID=""
  # ONE budget for the WHOLE call (see wait_for_cooldown): reset here, consumed across every retry.
  WAIT_SPENT_MS=0
  trap 'on_exit' EXIT
  # ⛔ THE CALLER'S TERM MUST REACH `gh`. A bare fork (the obvious way to read stderr) leaves the
  # child running when the caller kills us — through its retries, still hammering a bucket GitHub
  # has already refused, with nobody left to read the answer (the behaviour gh-limit.py:105 cites
  # from the API docs). Forward the signal to the child we are waiting on.
  trap 'on_signal' TERM INT HUP
  # An INTERACTIVE call (a human at a terminal: `gh auth login`, and `gh` prompts on STDERR while
  # READING STDIN) is paced like every other call but never retried and never captured — capturing
  # the prompt would leave the human staring at a blank screen while gh waits for input. Keyed on
  # STDIN, which is what gh reads: `gh pr create 2>log` also prompts, and would render invisibly on
  # the capture path.
  # ⛔ AND STDOUT IS CAPTURED, WHICH A HUMAN AT A TERMINAL CAN NOTICE: gh sees a FILE rather than a
  # terminal, so its own colour and pager autodetection are off, and the output arrives when the call
  # finishes instead of streaming. That is the price of a retry that does not duplicate output (see
  # the capture block below), and it is stated here rather than discovered. It does NOT change WHICH
  # calls are retried: a terminal on stdin still takes the un-retried streaming branch, exactly as it
  # did before this change (and it keeps the rate-limit wait it always had).
  if [ -t 0 ]; then interactive=1; fi
  if [ "$GATE_ON" -eq 0 ]; then
    gate_note "cannot use the gate directory ($GATE_DIR) — running UNGATED (announced); retries still apply"
  fi
  while :; do
    if [ "$GATE_ON" -eq 1 ]; then
      # ⛔ A DECLARED-BOUND CALLER IS NOT SLEPT INSIDE — NOT EVEN OUT OF A SHARED WINDOW. The
      # window is capped by RETRY_MAX (900 s by default), which is 3x the admission bound and far
      # past the 60-420 s budgets the NO_WAIT contract exists for, so waiting it out re-created the
      # STALL this gate is supposed to prevent. Such a caller asks immediately and gets the refusal
      # back; the window is still honoured by every caller that did not declare a bound.
      # The note is emitted ONLY when a window actually exists — saying "the account is in a
      # shared rate-limit window" on every NO_WAIT call would put a false statement in events.log,
      # which is the surface an operator debugs with.
      if no_wait_wanted; then
        if rem="$(cooldown_remaining_ms)"; then
          gate_note "the account is in a shared rate-limit window ($(( (rem + 999) / 1000 ))s left) — this call is NOT waiting it out (caller set NO_WAIT); it may be refused"
        fi
      else
        wait_for_cooldown; rc_wait=$?
        # ⛔ A CONTENDED CALL GETS A DISTINCT, GREPPABLE VERDICT — never a hang, and never an empty
        # success. `rc=75` (EX_TEMPFAIL) is not `rc=1`: a reader above can tell "I could not ask"
        # from "the answer is no", which is how a lane stops reporting "no PRs" when it simply
        # could not read. The budget was already spent, so returning is immediate.
        if [ "$rc_wait" -eq 3 ]; then
          # ⛔ THIS NOTE MUST NOT CONTAIN A RATE-LIMIT PHRASE. `gh-limit.py` reads this shim's
          # STDERR, and `classify()` treats any `LIMIT_PHRASES` term with no HTTP status as a
          # refusal (gh-limit.py:2998-3006). The first cut of this note said "shared rate-limit
          # window" and "secondary/abuse", so a CONTENDED verdict was re-classified as a LIMIT one
          # layer up: 4 retries, 60/120/240 s of plan_wait, and a 60 s+ `last-limit` marker that
          # blocked OTHER lanes for a refusal that never happened — the exact defect this change
          # exists to delete, reintroduced with a far larger ceiling (#3228 review, finding 1).
          # classify() now short-circuits rc=75 as well, so wording drift cannot recapture it;
          # both halves are required, and tests/gh-shim/run.sh pins them.
          gate_note "gh-contended — a shared gh throttle window outlived this call's ${WAIT_BUDGET_MS}ms wait budget and no exempt quota read cleared it (a burst/concurrency window is never discharged by one) — returning rc=75 rather than hanging into the caller's timeout"
          release_slot
          flush_notes 75
          cleanup_workdir
          exit 75
        fi
      fi
      if [ "$NESTED" -eq 0 ]; then
        admit_slot; adm=$?
        # The two fail-open exits are NOT the same event: 1 = the slot cap was reached after
        # ACQUIRE_TIMEOUT, 2 = the mutex itself could not be taken (~LOCK_TIMEOUT_MS). Reporting
        # both as "no slot free within 300s" sends an operator hunting a starvation that did not
        # happen, on the one surface (events.log) the runbook tells them to read.
        [ "$adm" -eq 2 ] && gate_note "the gate mutex is unavailable (lock_acquire spent ${LOCK_TIMEOUT_MS}ms) — running this call UNGATED (announced)"
        [ "$adm" -eq 1 ] && gate_note "no gh slot free within $(( ACQUIRE_TIMEOUT_MS / 1000 ))s — running this call UNGATED (announced)"
        # Another lane may have been refused while we queued for the slot. Honour its
        # window BEFORE asking: that re-check is what makes a burst one refusal, not N.
        if rem="$(cooldown_remaining_ms)"; then
          # ⛔ KEEP THE SLOT. Releasing it here let a NO_WAIT caller run UNGATED during a window,
          # so the concurrency cap was silently bypassed exactly when it mattered (measured: 3
          # concurrent children at MAX_INFLIGHT=1). Only the WAIT is suppressed for such a caller.
          no_wait_wanted || { release_slot; continue; }
        fi
      fi
      pace_tick
    fi
    [ -n "${MY_SLOT:-}" ] && export AGENT_GH_GATE_SLOT="$MY_SLOT"
    if [ "$interactive" -eq 1 ]; then
      # ⛔ NO NOTE BEFORE THE CHILD RUNS. This branch has no capture, but stderr is NOT
      # necessarily a terminal: `gh-rest/gh` reaches here as `run_real … 2>&1 | jq` with stdin
      # inherited from a human's tty, so printing the pending notes first contaminated a
      # SUCCESSFUL call's merged output (reproduced with a pty on stdin). Replay them after,
      # only if the call failed — identical to every other branch.
      "$REAL" "$@"
      rc=$?
      release_slot
      flush_notes "$rc"
      cleanup_workdir
      exit "$rc"
    fi
    # ⛔ THE CAPTURE FILE MUST NOT BE ABLE TO STOP THE CALL. bash performs a redirection BEFORE
    # exec, so `2>"$errf"` on an unusable path (read-only fs, FULL disk) means `gh` NEVER RUNS and
    # the caller gets rc=1 — a gate that is meant to be a courtesy turning its own disk problem
    # into a total gh outage, while the note above it claims the call ran ungated.
    if ! : 2>/dev/null > "$errf" || ! : 2>/dev/null > "$outf"; then
      gate_note "cannot create the capture files ($errf, $outf) — running this call UNGATED, with no retry"
      "$REAL" "$@"
      rc=$?
      release_slot
      flush_notes "$rc"
      cleanup_workdir
      exit "$rc"
    fi
    # ⛔ THE CAPTURE IS OWNER-ONLY (`0600`). A command that PRINTS A SECRET — `gh auth token` is the
    # one that matters — now has its stdout in a FILE for the life of the call, where it used to be
    # streamed. The parent gate dir is 0700; this narrows it again, and the exit trap / TERM/INT/HUP
    # remove the file on every path this layer controls. A SIGKILL leaves it behind, exactly as the
    # pre-existing STDERR capture already can (STATED LIMITS).
    chmod 600 "$outf" "$errf" 2>/dev/null
    # `<&0` is REQUIRED: a background job in a non-interactive shell is handed /dev/null for stdin
    # by POSIX, which would break `gh api --input -` (the documented REST fallback).
    # ⛔ STDOUT IS CAPTURED PER ATTEMPT, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING (review of #2768, P1). The failing
    # transport signatures here — `connection reset` and `unexpected eof` — are exactly the ones that
    # leave EARLIER BYTES on stdout. With stdout streamed straight to the caller, a retried
    # `gh api --paginate` returned rc=0 with page 1 from the FAILED attempt followed by the whole
    # successful answer (duplicated/half-written JSON), so `atomic-land.sh`'s tree count double-counts
    # and a `--json` reader gets a JSONDecodeError from a call that SUCCEEDED. Capturing means a
    # discarded attempt's output never reaches the caller; only the attempt whose rc we report is
    # replayed, below.
    "$REAL" "$@" >"$outf" 2>"$errf" <&0 &
    CHILD_PID=$!
    wait "$CHILD_PID"
    rc=$?
    CHILD_PID=""
    [ "$rc" -eq 0 ] && break
    # ── ARM 1: RATE LIMIT (unchanged) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    if [ "$tries" -lt $(( MAX_ATTEMPTS - 1 )) ] && rate_limited "$errf"; then
      tries=$(( tries + 1 ))
      wait_ms="$(retry_wait_ms "$errf" "$tries")"
      # Share the window with the WHOLE box before deciding whether to sleep in it.
      cd_until=""
      [ "$GATE_ON" -eq 1 ] && cd_until="$(set_cooldown_ms "$wait_ms")"
      # ⛔ RECORD THE KIND OF WINDOW, AND THE DEADLINE IT PROTECTS. A SECONDARY/ABUSE refusal is
      # invisible to the exempt quota read, so it must never be discharged by one (see
      # `secondary_live`). ⛔ AND NOTHING HERE CLEARS IT: this used to `rm` the flag on every
      # ordinary refusal, which un-protected a LONGER secondary window still governing the box —
      # deadlines are monotonic maxima, so the newest window is not the governing one, and the old
      # comment had that backwards (#3228 review, finding 2). The flag now expires by its own
      # deadline and by nothing else.
      # ⛔ THE SECONDARY PATTERNS ARE THE RUNBOOK'S OWN SYMPTOM, not a guess: `docs/ops/gh-rate-limits.md`
      # §"Two limits" names `API rate limit already exceeded for user ID …` as the secondary
      # signature and records that it strikes *while the primary counter reads healthy*. The word
      # that distinguishes it from a primary exhaustion is `already`, and leaving it out classified
      # exactly that refusal as PRIMARY — so the very read that cannot see a secondary limit
      # discharged it. `retry-after` is recorded too, on its own flag: it is the server's own
      # instruction and `gh-limit.py` refuses to discharge one (see `window_kind_live`).
      if [ "$GATE_ON" -eq 1 ]; then
        case "$cd_until" in
          ''|*[!0-9]*) : ;;
          *)
            if grep -Eqi 'secondary rate limit|abuse detection|api rate limit already exceeded' "$errf" 2>/dev/null; then
              printf '%s' "$cd_until" 2>/dev/null > "$GATE_DIR/secondary.window"
            fi
            if grep -Eqi 'retry-after:' "$errf" 2>/dev/null; then
              printf '%s' "$cd_until" 2>/dev/null > "$GATE_DIR/retry-after.window"
            fi ;;
        esac
      fi
      # ⛔ A CALLER THAT DECLARED ITS OWN BOUND IS NOT SLEPT INSIDE. gh-limit.py sets
      # GH_LIMIT_NO_WAIT=1 precisely so a caller with a 60-420 s timeout gets the refusal back AT
      # ONCE and can report RATE-LIMITED instead of STALL — a lower layer must not override the
      # upper layer's declared policy. The cooldown above is still written, so the box benefits.
      if no_wait_wanted; then
        gate_note "caller set NO_WAIT — returning the refusal at once instead of sleeping $(( (wait_ms + 999) / 1000 ))s (the cooldown is shared)"
        break
      fi
      release_slot
      gate_note "GitHub refused this call (rate limit) — waiting $(( (wait_ms + 999) / 1000 ))s, then retrying (retry $tries of $(( MAX_ATTEMPTS - 1 )))"
      sleep_ms "$wait_ms"
      continue
    fi
    # ── ARM 2: TRANSPORT (#2148) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    # `rate_limited` is re-tested here so a rate-limit refusal that has spent its own attempts is
    # NOT picked up by this arm: a refusal is a decision, and this arm exists for a dropped socket.
    if [ "$ttries" -lt $(( TRANSPORT_ATTEMPTS - 1 )) ] && treason="$(transport_reason "$errf" "$rc")" \
       && ! rate_limited "$errf" && read_only_call "$@"; then
      ttries=$(( ttries + 1 ))
      # 1 s, then 3 s — <= 4 s in total, and every value is a literal so no knob can unbound it.
      case "$ttries" in 1) wait_ms=1000 ;; *) wait_ms=3000 ;; esac
      # ⛔ NO COOLDOWN AND NO `no_wait_wanted` GATE. A transport blip is not a shared window, so
      # nothing here may park the box the way a rate-limit refusal does; and a 1 s/3 s backoff is
      # two orders below the 60-420 s budgets the NO_WAIT contract exists to protect, so suppressing
      # it would delete the fix for exactly the bounded callers that can least afford a dead read.
      transport_note "transport retry $ttries/$TRANSPORT_ATTEMPTS ($treason)"
      release_slot
      sleep_ms "$wait_ms"
      continue
    fi
    # Name the exhaustion — but ONLY when the call ACTUALLY died of transport, and only with the
    # attempt count that really happened (review of #2768, P2): a transport blip followed by an
    # honest `HTTP 422` used to print "exhausted after 3 attempts" for a 2-attempt call whose last
    # failure was GitHub answering. That is a false cause in the operator's diagnostic surface.
    if [ "$ttries" -gt 0 ] && transport_reason "$errf" "$rc" >/dev/null; then
      # ⛔ THE COUNT IS EVERY ATTEMPT THIS LOOP MADE, NOT THE TRANSPORT ONES. The two arms share this
      # loop, so a rate-limit retry before the transport ones made `ttries + 1` UNDERSTATE the real
      # count (measured: `ratelimit, connect, connect, connect` = 4 invocations reported as 3).
      transport_note "transport failure persisted after $(( tries + ttries + 1 )) attempt(s) — returning rc=$rc"
    fi
    break
  done
  release_slot
  # ⛔ THE ATTEMPT WHOSE RC WE REPORT IS THE ATTEMPT WHOSE OUTPUT THE CALLER GETS — once. Nothing is
  # replayed from a discarded attempt (see the capture comment above), and stdout keeps its own
  # stream so a `--jq`/`--json` reader above this layer sees exactly one document.
  [ -s "$outf" ] && cat "$outf" 2>/dev/null
  [ -s "$errf" ] && cat "$errf" >&2 2>/dev/null
  flush_notes "$rc"
  cleanup_workdir
  exit "$rc"
}

# Everything that is not an admin merge is not this shim's business.
if [ "$IS_ADMIN" -eq 0 ]; then
  run_paced "$@"
fi

# FROM HERE ON: an admin merge. Refusal is the default; the rail's evidence is the
# only thing that opens it.
if [ "${AGENT_ADMIN_MERGE_OVERRIDE:-}" = "1" ]; then
  printf 'gh-shim: ⚠️  ADMIN-MERGE OVERRIDE — AGENT_ADMIN_MERGE_OVERRIDE=1 (audited).\n' >&2
  printf 'gh-shim:    The post-merge detector still runs. Ensure the operator approved this.\n' >&2
  run_paced "$@"
fi

if [ -z "$PR_NUM" ]; then
  printf 'gh-shim: ✗ REFUSED — an admin merge with no PR number in argv cannot be justified.\n' >&2
  printf 'gh-shim:   Name the PR so its head-bound evidence can be checked, or use the rail:\n' >&2
  printf 'gh-shim:       scripts/admin-merge.sh <PR> [--workflow <lane>]\n' >&2
  exit 1
fi
# An UNRECOGNIZED flag before the positional means we cannot tell a flag's VALUE
# from the PR number (`--future-flag 123`). Fail closed rather than verify the
# wrong PR.
if [ -n "$saw_unknown_flag" ]; then
  printf 'gh-shim: ✗ REFUSED — unrecognized flag %s before the PR number, so the number\n' "$saw_unknown_flag" >&2
  printf 'gh-shim:   cannot be told from that flag'"'"'s value. Pass the PR first, or use the rail.\n' >&2
  exit 1
fi
# The positional is either a bare number or the one accepted PR URL (#1007).
case "$PR_NUM" in *[!0-9]*)
  if parsed="$(parse_pr_url "$PR_NUM")"; then
    # `parsed` is `<owner> <repo> <number>` — the repo is TWO fields joined by a
    # slash, not one (a `%% *` on the whole string returns the repo alone).
    parsed_rest="${parsed#* }"                      # `<repo> <number>`
    PR_URL_REPO="${parsed%% *}/${parsed_rest%% *}"   # `owner/repo`
    PR_NUM="${parsed_rest##* }"
  else
    printf 'gh-shim: ✗ REFUSED — %s is not a PR number or a github.com PR URL, so evidence cannot be resolved.\n' "$PR_NUM" >&2
    printf 'gh-shim:   The positional may be a number or https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>/pull/<n>.\n' >&2
    printf 'gh-shim:   A BRANCH name is deliberately NOT resolved: a gate does not ask gh who it is gating.\n' >&2
    exit 1
  fi ;;
esac
# A BARE number must satisfy the SAME rule `parse_pr_url` applies to the URL form: no
# leading zero, at most 15 digits. The scanner holds a JS `Number`, which normalises
# `01006` and ROUNDS above 2^53 (`9007199254740993` -> `...992`), while this side passes
# the literal digits to gh — so without this the two layers can name different PRs and
# the gate reads the wrong PR's evidence (fresh review, P2).
case "$PR_NUM" in
  ''|0*|*[!0-9]*)
    printf 'gh-shim: ✗ REFUSED — %s is not a PR number both this shim and the scanner can\n' "$PR_NUM" >&2
    printf 'gh-shim:   name exactly (no leading zero; digits only). Give the number GitHub prints.\n' >&2
    exit 1 ;;
esac
if [ "${#PR_NUM}" -gt 15 ]; then
  printf 'gh-shim: ✗ REFUSED — a %s-digit PR number cannot be held exactly by the scanner,\n' "${#PR_NUM}" >&2
  printf 'gh-shim:   so the two layers could name different PRs. Use the URL form or the rail.\n' >&2
  exit 1
fi

# A URL carries its OWN repo, and gh resolves the PR against the URL — it IGNORES a
# contradicting `--repo` (probed live 2026-09-14: `gh pr view <agent-infra URL> --repo
# <tortoise>` returned the agent-infra URL; `gh pr merge <URL to a repo that does not
# exist> --repo <one that does>` failed on the URL's repo, and the mirror image failed
# on the URL's PR and never on the bogus `--repo` — so `pr merge` and `pr view` share
# one lookup). The URL's repo is therefore what the evidence must be read from:
# dropping it would read the evidence from the CWD's repo, and honouring a
# contradicting `--repo` would verify a PR gh is not about to merge. The URL wins here
# for the same reason it wins in gh. `github.com/owner/repo` is the same repo as
# `owner/repo` (gh accepts the HOST/-prefixed form), so the host is dropped for the
# COMPARISON only — the value forwarded is unchanged.
if [ -n "$PR_URL_REPO" ]; then
  if [ -n "$REPO_FLAG" ] && [ "${REPO_FLAG#github.com/}" != "$PR_URL_REPO" ]; then
    printf 'gh-shim: note: --repo %s is ignored — gh resolves a PR URL against the repo named in the URL (%s).\n' \
      "$REPO_FLAG" "$PR_URL_REPO" >&2
  fi
  REPO_FLAG="$PR_URL_REPO"
fi

if [ ! -x "$VERIFY" ] && [ ! -f "$VERIFY" ]; then
  printf 'gh-shim: ✗ REFUSED — the evidence verifier is missing (%s).\n' "$VERIFY" >&2
  printf 'gh-shim:   Failing closed: an unverifiable admin merge does not happen.\n' >&2
  exit 1
fi

verify_args=("$PR_NUM")
[ -n "$REPO_FLAG" ] && verify_args+=(--repo "$REPO_FLAG")
verify_out="$(bash "$VERIFY" "${verify_args[@]}" 2>&1)"
verify_rc=$?
if [ "$verify_rc" -ne 0 ]; then
  printf '%s\n' "$verify_out" | sed 's/^/gh-shim: /' >&2
  printf 'gh-shim: ✗ REFUSED — no head-bound evidence justifies this admin merge (PR #%s).\n' "$PR_NUM" >&2
  printf 'gh-shim:   `--admin` bypasses required checks, so it needs the rail certificate:\n' >&2
  printf 'gh-shim:       scripts/admin-merge.sh %s [--workflow <lane>]\n' "$PR_NUM" >&2
  exit 1
fi

printf 'gh-shim: ✅ head-bound evidence verified for PR #%s — allowing the admin merge.\n' "$PR_NUM" >&2
# The merge itself is a `gh` call like any other: it is paced, and it is retried ONCE the
# rate-limit wait has elapsed rather than landing a refusal the operator has to re-run by
# hand (a rate-limit refusal happens before the merge, so the retry is not a double-merge).
run_paced "$@"
